//! The game's behaviour: a horizontal move, **two** coins, one goal, a jump,
//! the ground state that gives the jump a semantic basis (PRD §2 P1..P5), and
//! the game's **own persisted liveness record** (plan gap S1).
//!
//! The numbers below are the level's design, not the contract: a Developer is
//! expected to change them (and this file) as the round's increment.  Only the
//! type paths in [`crate::contract`] are frozen.
//!
//! Two properties are deliberate because the observation battery depends on
//! them:
//!
//! * **`move_dir` is a held level.**  The velocity is derived from it every
//!   frame, so writing `0` stops the player immediately and no inertia drifts
//!   (PRD P1).  Clearing it every frame is what SPIKE-1 measured as a 2 px move
//!   over 0.4 s.
//! * **The jump's edge is consumed by the game.**  `jump_pressed` is a level, so
//!   the game latches it: one press is one jump, and holding it does not bounce.
//!
//! ## The level (iteration 2: two coins, monotone counting — plan P2B)
//!
//! Two coins and a goal, all on the same ground plane, in increasing x.  Coin #1
//! and the goal keep iteration 1's positions, so the already-verified traces
//! stay the regression baseline; coin #2 is iteration 2's increment.
//!
//! The geometry is derived from iteration 1's own recorded drive of the
//! battery's session (`raw/e3_*.json`): the first held-right leg travels ~18
//! frames at ~3.4 px/frame and stops at x ~ 61, and the second leg passes
//! x ~ 100 by its first read.  Hence:
//!
//! ```text
//!   x = 0      player start
//!   x = 60     coin #1   reach 48..72: unchanged from iteration 1, so the first
//!                        leg definitely picks it up, while a read before any
//!                        input (x = 0) still sees `coins = 0`
//!   x = 90     coin #2   reach 78..102: the first leg stops ~17 px short of it,
//!                        so the read right after that leg is exactly 1; the
//!                        second leg walks straight through it (x ~ 78 within a
//!                        handful of frames), long before the goal, so the next
//!                        read is exactly 2
//!   x = 100    goal      unchanged from iteration 1, and past both coins, so the
//!                        win's false -> true happens *after* the second pickup,
//!                        at the very place iteration 1 already recorded
//! ```
//!
//! Walking right therefore takes the frozen `CoinCounter` 0 -> 1 -> 2 and only
//! then flips `WinFlag` false -> true, in that order, in one session — which is
//! exactly what this iteration's priority 3 re-proves in a single fresh headless
//! run.  The two reach windows are disjoint (48..72 and 78..102), which is what
//! keeps "1 after the first coin" and "2 after the second" separate readings
//! instead of one saturated number.
//!
//! A coin is a **real entity** with a `Transform`, and the pickup is a proximity
//! test against the player's x: touching a coin marks *that* coin collected and
//! increments the frozen counter once.  The per-coin `collected` flag is what
//! makes the count monotone: standing still on an already-collected coin, or
//! walking back past it, never counts it again.  The pickup radius is wider than
//! one frame's step at [`MAX_STEP_SECONDS`], so a slow frame cannot tunnel the
//! player past a coin.
//!
//! ## The win is on the walkable path (this iteration's priority 2)
//!
//! `GOAL_X` is past both coins on the *same* ground plane the player walks on,
//! and `detect_win` fires on `player_x >= goal_x` with the player standing
//! (`Grounded.on_ground` is true on the frame the flag flips, because a walk has
//! no airborne frames).  No new reflected type was added for the goal: the
//! frozen contract exposes no goal marker or goal x, so the acceptance gate
//! drops the "player x equals the goal's reachable x" equality sub-condition
//! rather than inventing a contract change.  What the game *can* do is keep the
//! goal unambiguously reachable by walking right, and both the unit tests below
//! and the battery's `e3_win_position` step assert that.
//!
//! ## The persisted liveness record (plan gap S1)
//!
//! `liveness_probe` appends one self-describing record per wall-clock deadline
//! (2 s, 4 s, 8 s, 15 s, 25 s, 40 s, 60 s after launch, then every 30 s) to
//! `.hoh/deterministic/raw/liveness.jsonl` **and** `…/liveness.log` under the
//! round's artifact directory ([`ARTIFACT_DIR_ENV`]), and echoes it on stderr.
//!
//! The record is stamped with the *game's own* [`FrameCounter`], never a poll
//! ordinal, and it carries the player transform, the ground state, the coin
//! counter and the win flag, so a reader can cite "this process was still
//! advancing frames `k` seconds after launch, with these values" long after the
//! short E3 battery window is over.  Wall-clock scheduling (not frame numbers)
//! is what makes a **late** record exist even if frames run far behind wall
//! time; the frame numbers in the record then prove the game really advanced.
//! Every failure mode is swallowed: a liveness probe must never be able to
//! stall, panic or fail the game it is proving alive.
//!
//! The frame order is `count_frames -> apply_input -> integrate ->
//! collect_coins -> detect_win -> liveness_probe`, so the whole of one frame's
//! motion happens before the coin/goal test and every value the game persists or
//! a tool can read is self-consistent at the end of the frame.
//!
//! The maths of one frame lives in small pure helpers (`plan_velocity`,
//! `step_motion`, `collects_at`, `win_reached`) that the systems are thin
//! wrappers around.  That is what makes the physics and the pickup testable
//! without a clock: the tests at the bottom of this file drive the very same
//! code the running game drives, with a fixed `dt` of one 60 Hz frame.

use bevy::prelude::*;

use crate::contract::{CoinCounter, FrameCounter, Grounded, InputIntent, Player, Velocity, WinFlag};

/// The ground plane's height.  Also the player's and every coin's height.
pub const GROUND_Y: f32 = -200.0;
/// Horizontal speed, in pixels per second.
pub const MOVE_SPEED: f32 = 200.0;
/// The upwards impulse, in pixels per second.
pub const JUMP_SPEED: f32 = 300.0;
/// Downwards acceleration, in pixels per second squared.
pub const GRAVITY: f32 = 1200.0;
/// The first coin's x.  Iteration 1's coin, unchanged; it is the first entry of
/// [`COIN_XS`], the authoritative list.
pub const COIN_X: f32 = 60.0;
/// The second coin's x: beyond the first, and clear of where the battery's first
/// held-right leg stops, so the counter has to go 0 -> 1 -> 2 over two legs
/// instead of saturating on the first pickup.
pub const COIN2_X: f32 = 90.0;
/// The coin positions on the x axis, in the order they are meant to be reached.
pub const COIN_XS: [f32; 2] = [COIN_X, COIN2_X];
/// How close the player's x has to be to a coin's x to pick it up.
///
/// At 60 Hz the player advances ~3.4 px per frame (at most one
/// [`MAX_STEP_SECONDS`] step, i.e. ~6.7 px), so a radius wider than one frame's
/// step is what makes the pickup impossible to skip over.  With this radius the
/// two coins' reach windows (48..72 and 78..102) are disjoint, which is what
/// keeps "1 after the first coin" and "2 after the second" separate readings.
pub const COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS: f32 = 12.0;
/// Where the goal sits on the x axis.  Iteration 1's position, unchanged: past
/// both coins on the same ground plane, so the coin count is already 2 when the
/// flag flips, and at the exact place whose win frame iteration 1 already proved
/// reachable.  It stays on the walkable path — no jump is needed to win.
pub const GOAL_X: f32 = 100.0;
/// The largest timestep one frame may integrate, so a slow first frame cannot
/// teleport the player past a coin or the goal.
pub const MAX_STEP_SECONDS: f32 = 1.0 / 30.0;

/// Wall-clock deadlines (milliseconds after launch) at which [`liveness_probe`]
/// persists a record.  The early ones land inside the short E3 battery window;
/// the later ones (`25 s`, `40 s`, `60 s`) are the *late* records gap S1 asks
/// for, and they exist no matter how the frame rate behaves, because the
/// schedule is wall time and not frame counts.
pub const LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS: [u64; 7] = [2_000, 4_000, 8_000, 15_000, 25_000, 40_000, 60_000];
/// Once the fixed deadlines are spent, records repeat at this interval, so a
/// long-lived session keeps being evidenced instead of going quiet after 60 s.
pub const LIVENESS_INTERVAL_MS: u64 = 30_000;
/// The evidence directory the records are persisted into, relative to the
/// working directory.
pub const LIVENESS_DIR_RELATIVE: &str = ".hoh/deterministic/raw";
/// The machine-readable record file (one JSON object per line).
pub const LIVENESS_JSONL_NAME: &str = "liveness.jsonl";
/// The human-readable record file (continues iteration 2's `liveness.log`).
pub const LIVENESS_LOG_NAME: &str = "liveness.log";

/// The launcher's environment variable naming the round's artifact directory.
///
/// When the game is launched with it set, the liveness records are written under
/// `<artifact>/deterministic/raw/` instead of relative to the working directory,
/// so the record lands in the round's own evidence area even if the process was
/// started from somewhere else.
pub const ARTIFACT_DIR_ENV: &str = "HOH_ARTIFACT_DIR";

/// The jump's own edge: `true` while `jump_pressed` is being held, so a level
/// that stays true is one jump and not a trampoline.
#[derive(Component, Debug, Default)]
struct JumpLatch(bool);

/// A pickable coin.  Private on purpose: the coin's *consequence* is the frozen
/// [`CoinCounter`], and nothing outside the game acts on a coin entity.
#[derive(Component, Debug, Clone, Copy)]
struct Coin {
    /// `true` once this coin has been picked up; a coin counts exactly once.
    collected: bool,
}

/// The goal post.  Also private: its consequence is the frozen [`WinFlag`].
#[derive(Component, Debug, Default)]
struct Goal;

/// The schedule of persisted liveness records (plan gap S1).
///
/// It is a *wall-clock* schedule: `started` is taken when the game boots and
/// every record is due at a fixed number of milliseconds after it, so the record
/// a reader cites for "still alive late in the round" exists regardless of how
/// many frames ran in between.  `written` is how many deadlines have been
/// served; [`next_deadline_ms`] turns it back into the next due time.
///
/// Nothing in the game derives behaviour from it — it only observes.
#[derive(Resource, Debug)]
struct LivenessClock {
    started: std::time::Instant,
    written: u64,
}

impl Default for LivenessClock {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self {
            started: std::time::Instant::now(),
            written: 0,
        }
    }
}

pub fn add(app: &mut App) {
    app.init_resource::<LivenessClock>();
    app.add_systems(Startup, (spawn_scene, announce_boot));
    app.add_systems(
        Update,
        (
            count_frames,
            apply_input,
            integrate,
            collect_coins,
            detect_win,
            liveness_probe,
        )
            .chain(),
    );
}

fn spawn_scene(mut commands: Commands) {
    // The player: the frozen marker, an engine `Transform`, the ground state and
    // the two game-owned pieces of state (`contract::Velocity` is registered so
    // the wire can localise a movement failure; `JumpLatch` stays private
    // because nothing outside the game can act on it).
    commands.spawn((
        Player,
        Transform::from_xyz(0.0, GROUND_Y, 0.0),
        Grounded::default(),
        Velocity::default(),
        JumpLatch::default(),
    ));

    // The coins: real entities, reachable on foot, each counted at most once.
    for coin_x in COIN_XS {
        commands.spawn((
            Coin { collected: false },
            Transform::from_xyz(coin_x, GROUND_Y, 0.0),
        ));
    }

    // The goal post, past both coins on the same ground plane.
    commands.spawn((Goal, Transform::from_xyz(GOAL_X, GROUND_Y, 0.0)));

    // The level's own tally: two coins to find.  `main` installed the contract
    // default (0 of 1) at startup; the level owns the real number, so the seed
    // is written here, next to the level that defines it.  `coins` itself still
    // starts at 0 (PRD P2).
    commands.insert_resource(CoinCounter {
        coins: 0,
        target: COIN_XS.len() as i64,
    });
}

/// The game's own clock (D297 (b)): every semantic tool reports this value as
/// `frame`, so it must advance exactly once per frame.
fn count_frames(mut counter: ResMut<FrameCounter>) {
    counter.frames = counter.frames.saturating_add(1);
}

/// Turn the held intent into velocity, and consume the jump's edge.
fn apply_input(
    intent: Res<InputIntent>,
    mut player: Query<(&mut Velocity, &mut Grounded, &mut JumpLatch), With<Player>>,
) {
    for (mut velocity, mut grounded, mut latch) in &mut player {
        plan_velocity(&mut velocity, &mut grounded, &mut latch, *intent);
    }
}

/// The input half of one frame, as a pure function.
///
/// `velocity.x` is **recomputed from the level** every frame, so writing
/// `move_dir = 0` stops the player on that frame and no inertia is carried over
/// (PRD P1).  The jump is an **edge** even though `jump_pressed` is a level: the
/// latch remembers that the press was already spent, so holding the key is one
/// jump and not a trampoline.
fn plan_velocity(
    velocity: &mut Velocity,
    grounded: &mut Grounded,
    latch: &mut JumpLatch,
    intent: InputIntent,
) {
    velocity.x = MOVE_SPEED * intent.move_dir as f32;
    if intent.jump_pressed && grounded.on_ground && !latch.0 {
        velocity.y = JUMP_SPEED;
        grounded.on_ground = false;
    }
    latch.0 = intent.jump_pressed;
}

/// Integrate one frame of motion and settle on the ground.
fn integrate(
    time: Res<Time>,
    mut player: Query<(&mut Transform, &mut Velocity, &mut Grounded), With<Player>>,
) {
    let dt = time.delta_secs().min(MAX_STEP_SECONDS);
    for (mut transform, mut velocity, mut grounded) in &mut player {
        step_motion(&mut transform, &mut velocity, &mut grounded, dt);
    }
}

/// The motion half of one frame, as a pure function.
///
/// Gravity is applied *before* the position update, and the ground is a clamp:
/// `y` can never go below [`GROUND_Y`], the downwards velocity is zeroed on
/// contact, and `Grounded.on_ground` is true exactly while standing.
fn step_motion(
    transform: &mut Transform,
    velocity: &mut Velocity,
    grounded: &mut Grounded,
    dt: f32,
) {
    transform.translation.x += velocity.x * dt;
    velocity.y -= GRAVITY * dt;
    transform.translation.y += velocity.y * dt;
    if transform.translation.y <= GROUND_Y {
        transform.translation.y = GROUND_Y;
        velocity.y = 0.0;
        grounded.on_ground = true;
    } else {
        grounded.on_ground = false;
    }
}

/// The pickup test, as a pure function: is the player within reach of a coin
/// that sits at `coin_x`?
fn collects_at(coin_x: f32, player_x: f32) -> bool {
    (player_x - coin_x).abs() <= COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS
}

/// The win test, as a pure function: has the player walked on to the goal?
fn win_reached(goal_x: f32, player_x: f32) -> bool {
    player_x >= goal_x
}

/// Touch a coin, incrementing the frozen counter once per coin (PRD P2).
///
/// The per-coin `collected` flag is the whole reason the counter is monotone:
/// the increment can only ever happen on the transition of one specific coin
/// from "untouched" to "touched", so standing on a coin, or walking back over
/// it, counts nothing.  That is iteration 2's plan requirement ("no re-trigger
/// when the player stands still on an already-collected coin").
fn collect_coins(
    mut counter: ResMut<CoinCounter>,
    player: Query<&Transform, With<Player>>,
    mut coins: Query<(&Transform, &mut Coin), Without<Player>>,
) {
    for player_transform in &player {
        let player_x = player_transform.translation.x;
        for (coin_transform, mut coin) in &mut coins {
            if !coin.collected && collects_at(coin_transform.translation.x, player_x) {
                coin.collected = true;
                counter.coins = counter.coins.saturating_add(1);
            }
        }
    }
}

/// Reaching the goal latches the win flag (PRD P3).
///
/// The write is **one-way**: `won` is only ever set, never cleared, so the
/// false -> true transition the criterion looks for can never be undone.  The
/// goal test is *independent* of the coins -- PRD P3 asks that reaching the goal
/// flips the flag, so a run that walks straight there must still win, and the
/// level simply places the goal past both coins so the natural walk collects
/// them on the way.
fn detect_win(
    mut win: ResMut<WinFlag>,
    player: Query<&Transform, With<Player>>,
    goal: Query<&Transform, With<Goal>>,
) {
    if win.won {
        return;
    }
    for player_transform in &player {
        let player_x = player_transform.translation.x;
        for goal_transform in &goal {
            if win_reached(goal_transform.translation.x, player_x) {
                win.won = true;
            }
        }
    }
}

/// The next wall-clock deadline (milliseconds after launch) that has not been
/// served yet, given `written` records already written.
///
/// The first [`LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS`] records use the fixed early schedule;
/// after that the interval is [`LIVENESS_INTERVAL_MS`].  Pure, so the schedule
/// is testable without waiting for it.
fn next_deadline_ms(written: u64) -> u64 {
    let fixed = LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS.len() as u64;
    if written < fixed {
        LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS[written as usize]
    } else {
        let last = LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS[LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS.len() - 1];
        last + LIVENESS_INTERVAL_MS * (written - fixed + 1)
    }
}

/// How many deadlines have come due by `elapsed_ms`, never fewer than `written`.
///
/// Computed in closed form rather than by stepping deadline by deadline, so a
/// process that was suspended for an hour (or a clock that jumped) cannot turn
/// this into a long loop.  Pure, so the catch-up rule is unit tested.
fn records_due(written: u64, elapsed_ms: u64) -> u64 {
    let fixed = LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS.len();
    let last = LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS[fixed - 1];
    let due = if elapsed_ms < last {
        LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS
            .iter()
            .filter(|deadline| **deadline <= elapsed_ms)
            .count() as u64
    } else {
        fixed as u64 + (elapsed_ms - last) / LIVENESS_INTERVAL_MS
    };
    due.max(written)
}

/// Resolve the liveness evidence directory, preferring [`ARTIFACT_DIR_ENV`] when
/// it is set.  Kept pure (the environment is passed in) so it is testable.
fn liveness_dir(artifact_dir: Option<&str>) -> std::path::PathBuf {
    let relative = std::path::Path::new(LIVENESS_DIR_RELATIVE);
    let tail = relative.strip_prefix(".hoh").unwrap_or(relative);
    match artifact_dir {
        Some(dir) if !dir.is_empty() => std::path::Path::new(dir).join(tail),
        _ => relative.to_path_buf(),
    }
}

/// The evidence directory for *this* process: [`liveness_dir`] fed from the
/// environment.
fn resolved_liveness_dir() -> std::path::PathBuf {
    liveness_dir(std::env::var(ARTIFACT_DIR_ENV).ok().as_deref())
}

/// Append one line to `path`, creating it if needed, swallowing every error.
fn append_line(path: &std::path::Path, line: &str) {
    use std::io::Write;
    if let Ok(mut file) = std::fs::OpenOptions::new()
        .create(true)
        .append(true)
        .open(path)
    {
        let _ = writeln!(file, "{line}");
    }
}

/// Persist one record: the JSON line into [`LIVENESS_JSONL_NAME`] and the
/// `key=value` line into [`LIVENESS_LOG_NAME`].
///
/// Both sinks are attempted independently and neither can fail the caller: a
/// missing directory, a full disk or a locked file is ignored, because a
/// liveness probe must never be able to stall or fail the game it is proving
/// alive.
fn persist_record(dir: &std::path::Path, json: &str, text: &str) {
    if std::fs::create_dir_all(dir).is_err() && !dir.is_dir() {
        return;
    }
    append_line(&dir.join(LIVENESS_JSONL_NAME), json);
    append_line(&dir.join(LIVENESS_LOG_NAME), text);
}

/// The wall-clock milliseconds of "now", or 0 if the system clock is before the
/// epoch (which cannot fail the game either).
fn wall_ms() -> u128 {
    std::time::SystemTime::now()
        .duration_since(std::time::UNIX_EPOCH)
        .map(|elapsed| elapsed.as_millis())
        .unwrap_or(0)
}

/// The one line that says the process booted, written before the first frame.
///
/// It carries the game's frame counter and the process id, so the `raw/`
/// evidence area starts with a record proving *this* process reached startup.
fn announce_boot(frames: Res<FrameCounter>) {
    let pid = std::process::id();
    let wall = wall_ms();
    let json = format!(
        "{{\"probe\":\"hof_game\",\"event\":\"boot\",\"frame\":{},\"pid\":{},\"wall_ms\":{}}}",
        frames.frames, pid, wall
    );
    let text = format!(
        "hof_game boot frame={} pid={} wall_ms={}",
        frames.frames, pid, wall
    );
    eprintln!("{text}");
    persist_record(&resolved_liveness_dir(), &json, &text);
}

/// The persisted liveness record (plan gap S1).
///
/// Due on a **wall-clock** schedule ([`next_deadline_ms`]), so a record exists
/// seconds after launch whatever the frame rate does, and stamped with the
/// game's own [`FrameCounter`], so a reader can prove the game advanced rather
/// than the poller repeating itself.  It also carries the player transform, the
/// ground state, the coin counter and the win flag, which is what makes a late
/// record citable as "alive *and* still in the state the battery left it in".
///
/// Every failure mode is swallowed on purpose.  It observes; it never changes
/// behaviour.
fn liveness_probe(
    frames: Res<FrameCounter>,
    coins: Res<CoinCounter>,
    win: Res<WinFlag>,
    player: Query<(&Transform, &Grounded), With<Player>>,
    mut clock: ResMut<LivenessClock>,
) {
    let elapsed_ms = clock.started.elapsed().as_millis().min(u64::MAX as u128) as u64;
    let due = records_due(clock.written, elapsed_ms);
    if due <= clock.written {
        return;
    }
    // A process that was suspended for a long time has several deadlines behind
    // it; one record catches the schedule up (the values would be identical
    // anyway) instead of writing a burst of stale lines.
    clock.written = due;

    let pid = std::process::id();
    let wall = wall_ms();
    let frame = frames.frames;
    let player_state = player
        .iter()
        .next()
        .map(|(transform, grounded)| {
            (
                transform.translation.x,
                transform.translation.y,
                grounded.on_ground,
            )
        })
        .unwrap_or((f32::NAN, f32::NAN, false));
    let (x, y, on_ground) = player_state;

    let json = format!(
        "{{\"probe\":\"hof_game\",\"event\":\"liveness\",\"frame\":{frame},\"pid\":{pid},\
         \"wall_ms\":{wall},\"elapsed_ms\":{elapsed_ms},\"coins\":{},\"target\":{},\
         \"won\":{},\"x\":{x:.2},\"y\":{y:.2},\"on_ground\":{on_ground}}}",
        coins.coins, coins.target, win.won
    );
    let text = format!(
        "hof_game liveness frame={frame} pid={pid} wall_ms={wall} elapsed_ms={elapsed_ms} \
         coins={} target={} won={} x={x:.2} y={y:.2} on_ground={on_ground}",
        coins.coins, coins.target, win.won
    );
    eprintln!("{text}");
    persist_record(&resolved_liveness_dir(), &json, &text);
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    /// One 60 Hz game frame, the rate `main`'s schedule runner is set to.
    const DT: f32 = 1.0 / 60.0;

    /// The level from [`spawn_scene`], minus the ECS: the tests drive the very
    /// same helpers (`plan_velocity`, `step_motion`, `collects_at`,
    /// `win_reached`) the running systems call, so a behaviour that passes here
    /// is the behaviour the game has, not a copy of it.
    struct Sim {
        transform: Transform,
        velocity: Velocity,
        grounded: Grounded,
        latch: JumpLatch,
        coins: Vec<(f32, bool)>,
        counter: CoinCounter,
        win: WinFlag,
    }

    impl Sim {
        fn new() -> Self {
            Self {
                transform: Transform::from_xyz(0.0, GROUND_Y, 0.0),
                velocity: Velocity::default(),
                grounded: Grounded::default(),
                latch: JumpLatch::default(),
                coins: COIN_XS.iter().map(|x| (*x, false)).collect(),
                counter: CoinCounter {
                    coins: 0,
                    target: COIN_XS.len() as i64,
                },
                win: WinFlag::default(),
            }
        }

        /// One frame of the same pipeline the running game runs, in the same
        /// order: input, motion, pickup, goal.
        fn frame(&mut self, intent: InputIntent) {
            plan_velocity(&mut self.velocity, &mut self.grounded, &mut self.latch, intent);
            step_motion(&mut self.transform, &mut self.velocity, &mut self.grounded, DT);
            self.collect();
            self.reach_goal();
        }

        /// The pickup half of one frame, exactly like `collect_coins`.
        fn collect(&mut self) {
            let player_x = self.transform.translation.x;
            for (coin_x, collected) in &mut self.coins {
                if !*collected && collects_at(*coin_x, player_x) {
                    *collected = true;
                    self.counter.coins = self.counter.coins.saturating_add(1);
                }
            }
        }

        /// The goal half of one frame, exactly like `detect_win` (one-way).
        fn reach_goal(&mut self) {
            if !self.win.won && win_reached(GOAL_X, self.transform.translation.x) {
                self.win.won = true;
            }
        }
    }

    fn right() -> InputIntent {
        InputIntent {
            move_dir: 1,
            jump_pressed: false,
        }
    }

    fn left() -> InputIntent {
        InputIntent {
            move_dir: -1,
            jump_pressed: false,
        }
    }

    fn stop() -> InputIntent {
        InputIntent {
            move_dir: 0,
            jump_pressed: false,
        }
    }

    fn hold_jump() -> InputIntent {
        InputIntent {
            move_dir: 0,
            jump_pressed: true,
        }
    }

    #[test]
    fn held_input_moves_and_release_stops_without_inertia() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        for _ in 0..30 {
            sim.frame(right());
        }
        let moved = sim.transform.translation.x;
        assert!(
            moved > 90.0 && moved < 110.0,
            "30 frames at 200 px/s is 100 px, got {moved}"
        );

        // Release: level 0 must zero the velocity on the very first frame.
        for _ in 0..30 {
            sim.frame(stop());
        }
        assert_eq!(
            sim.transform.translation.x, moved,
            "released input must freeze x"
        );
        assert_eq!(sim.velocity.x, 0.0);
    }

    #[test]
    fn jump_rises_then_falls_and_lands() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        assert!(sim.grounded.on_ground, "the player stands before the press");

        let take_off_y = sim.transform.translation.y;

        // The very frame the press arrives: ground state off, rising.
        plan_velocity(
            &mut sim.velocity,
            &mut sim.grounded,
            &mut sim.latch,
            hold_jump(),
        );
        assert!(!sim.grounded.on_ground, "the press must clear the ground state");
        step_motion(&mut sim.transform, &mut sim.velocity, &mut sim.grounded, DT);
        assert!(
            sim.transform.translation.y > take_off_y,
            "the first airborne frame must already be higher"
        );

        // Keep holding: the latch means no second jump, and the player tracks a
        // full arc back to the ground.
        let mut trace = vec![sim.transform.translation.y];
        for _ in 0..60 {
            sim.frame(hold_jump());
            trace.push(sim.transform.translation.y);
        }

        let apex = trace.iter().cloned().fold(f32::MIN, f32::max);
        assert!(apex > take_off_y + 30.0, "the jump must rise: apex {apex}");
        let apex_at = trace.iter().position(|y| *y == apex).unwrap();
        assert!(apex_at > 0, "the apex is not the first frame");
        assert!(
            trace[apex_at..].iter().any(|y| *y < apex - 5.0),
            "the jump must come back down"
        );
        assert!(
            (trace[trace.len() - 1] - take_off_y).abs() < f32::EPSILON,
            "the jump must land back on the ground"
        );
        assert!(sim.grounded.on_ground, "and the ground state must be true again");
    }

    #[test]
    fn grounded_is_false_for_the_airborne_frames() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        // Rise phase: the first ~10 frames are still going up, so the player is
        // off the ground for all of them.
        sim.frame(hold_jump());
        for _ in 0..8 {
            assert!(
                !sim.grounded.on_ground,
                "the player is airborne while rising, y = {}",
                sim.transform.translation.y
            );
            assert!(sim.transform.translation.y > GROUND_Y);
            sim.frame(hold_jump());
        }
    }

    #[test]
    fn held_jump_is_one_jump_not_a_trampoline() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        let mut take_offs = 0;
        for _ in 0..120 {
            let was_grounded = sim.grounded.on_ground;
            plan_velocity(
                &mut sim.velocity,
                &mut sim.grounded,
                &mut sim.latch,
                hold_jump(),
            );
            if was_grounded && !sim.grounded.on_ground {
                take_offs += 1;
            }
            step_motion(&mut sim.transform, &mut sim.velocity, &mut sim.grounded, DT);
        }
        assert_eq!(take_offs, 1, "a held press is exactly one jump");
    }

    #[test]
    fn walking_right_collects_both_coins_monotonically() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 0, "the run starts at 0 coins");
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.target, 2, "the level holds two coins");

        let mut trace = Vec::new();
        let mut first_coin_at = None;
        let mut second_coin_at = None;
        for frame_index in 0..120 {
            sim.frame(right());
            trace.push(sim.counter.coins);
            if first_coin_at.is_none() && sim.counter.coins >= 1 {
                first_coin_at = Some(frame_index);
                assert!(sim.transform.translation.x >= COIN_X - COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS);
            }
            if second_coin_at.is_none() && sim.counter.coins >= 2 {
                second_coin_at = Some(frame_index);
                assert!(sim.transform.translation.x >= COIN2_X - COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS);
            }
        }

        let first = first_coin_at.expect("coin #1 is reachable on foot");
        let second = second_coin_at.expect("coin #2 is reachable on foot");
        assert!(first < second, "the coins are reached in their x order");
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 2, "two coins, counted twice");

        // Monotone: the sequence never decreases and steps one at a time.
        for pair in trace.windows(2) {
            assert!(pair[1] >= pair[0], "the count must never decrease: {trace:?}");
            assert!(pair[1] - pair[0] <= 1, "at most one coin per frame: {trace:?}");
        }

        // Walking back left never clears either value.
        for _ in 0..120 {
            sim.frame(left());
        }
        assert!(sim.transform.translation.x < COIN_X, "the player walked back");
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 2, "the coin counter never decreases");
    }

    #[test]
    fn the_first_leg_reads_one_and_the_second_leg_reads_two() {
        // The shape of the battery's drive: hold right, stop and read, hold
        // right again, read.  The stop lands inside coin #1's reach and clear of
        // coin #2's, so the read between the legs is exactly 1, and the second
        // leg walks through coin #2, making the next read 2.
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        for _ in 0..18 {
            sim.frame(right());
        }
        sim.frame(stop());
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 1, "the read after the first leg is 1");
        assert!(
            sim.transform.translation.x < COIN2_X - COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS,
            "x = {} must be short of coin #2's reach",
            sim.transform.translation.x
        );
        assert!(
            sim.transform.translation.x >= COIN_X - COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS,
            "x = {} must be inside coin #1's reach",
            sim.transform.translation.x
        );

        // The battery's idle wait must not change it.
        for _ in 0..10 {
            sim.frame(stop());
        }
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 1, "standing still must not re-count");

        for _ in 0..15 {
            sim.frame(right());
        }
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 2, "the read after the second leg is 2");
        assert!(sim.win.won, "and the same leg walks on to the goal");
        assert!(sim.grounded.on_ground, "reached standing on the ground");
    }

    #[test]
    fn one_walk_right_runs_the_whole_loop_in_one_session() {
        // This iteration's priority 3, as a test: a *single* continuous walk
        // right must take `coins` 0 -> 1 -> 2 and then flip `won` false -> true,
        // in that order, without a jump and without leaving the ground.  That is
        // what rules out "green while coins read 0".
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 0, "the run starts at 0 coins");
        assert!(!sim.win.won, "the run starts without the win");

        let mut first_pickup = None;
        let mut second_pickup = None;
        let mut won_at = None;
        for frame_index in 0..180 {
            sim.frame(right());
            if first_pickup.is_none() && sim.counter.coins == 1 {
                first_pickup = Some(frame_index);
            }
            if second_pickup.is_none() && sim.counter.coins == 2 {
                second_pickup = Some(frame_index);
            }
            if won_at.is_none() && sim.win.won {
                won_at = Some(frame_index);
            }
        }

        let first = first_pickup.expect("the walk reaches coin #1");
        let second = second_pickup.expect("the walk reaches coin #2");
        let win = won_at.expect("the walk reaches the goal");
        assert!(first < second, "coin #1 comes first");
        assert!(
            second <= win,
            "the win cannot be reached before the second coin: {second} > {win}"
        );
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 2, "both coins are counted");
        assert!(sim.win.won, "and the flag is true at the end of the walk");
        assert!(
            sim.transform.translation.x >= GOAL_X,
            "the player is at the goal: x = {}",
            sim.transform.translation.x
        );
        assert!(
            sim.grounded.on_ground,
            "the win happens standing on the walkable ground, no jump needed"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn standing_still_on_a_collected_coin_does_not_retrigger_it() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        // Walk on to the first coin and stop exactly on it.
        while sim.transform.translation.x < COIN_X {
            sim.frame(right());
        }
        sim.frame(stop());
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 1, "the first coin is counted once");

        // One second of standing exactly on the coin with the input released.
        for _ in 0..60 {
            sim.frame(stop());
        }
        assert_eq!(
            sim.counter.coins, 1,
            "standing still on a collected coin must not count it again"
        );
        assert!(
            collects_at(COIN_X, sim.transform.translation.x),
            "the player is still within reach of coin #1"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn the_goal_is_past_both_coins_and_the_win_is_one_way() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        assert!(!sim.win.won, "the run starts without the win");
        assert!(GOAL_X > COIN2_X, "the goal is past both coins");

        let mut won_at = None;
        for frame_index in 0..120 {
            sim.frame(right());
            if won_at.is_none() && sim.win.won {
                won_at = Some(frame_index);
                assert_eq!(
                    sim.counter.coins, 2,
                    "the win is only reachable after both coins"
                );
                assert!(sim.transform.translation.x >= GOAL_X);
            }
        }
        assert!(won_at.is_some(), "walking right must reach the goal");
        assert!(sim.grounded.on_ground, "and reach it standing on the ground");

        // One-way: walking back left never clears the flag.
        for _ in 0..120 {
            sim.frame(left());
        }
        assert!(sim.transform.translation.x < COIN_X, "the player did walk back");
        assert!(sim.win.won, "the win flag is one-way");
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 2, "and the count stays put");
    }

    #[test]
    fn nothing_is_collected_at_the_starting_position() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        assert!(!collects_at(COIN_X, 0.0), "the start is clear of coin #1");
        assert!(!collects_at(COIN2_X, 0.0), "the start is clear of coin #2");
        assert!(!win_reached(GOAL_X, 0.0), "the start is short of the goal");
        sim.frame(stop());
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 0, "the run starts at 0 coins");
        assert!(!sim.win.won, "the run starts without the win");
    }

    #[test]
    fn the_two_coin_windows_are_disjoint_and_ordered() {
        // The whole point of the geometry: coin #1's reach window ends before
        // coin #2's begins, so no single position collects both at once and the
        // counter has to step 0 -> 1 -> 2.
        let coin1_far = COIN_X + COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS;
        let coin2_near = COIN2_X - COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS;
        assert!(
            coin1_far < coin2_near,
            "coin #1 reach ends at {coin1_far}, coin #2 begins at {coin2_near}"
        );
        assert!(COIN2_X > COIN_X, "the coins are ordered left to right");
        assert!(GOAL_X > COIN2_X, "the goal is beyond coin #2");
        assert!(
            COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS > MOVE_SPEED * MAX_STEP_SECONDS,
            "one frame's step must be smaller than the reach window"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn the_liveness_dir_prefers_the_rounds_artifact_dir() {
        // The launcher exports HOH_ARTIFACT_DIR; when it is there the records
        // must land under that directory's `deterministic/raw/`, and when it is
        // not the working-directory-relative path is used.  The liveness
        // evidence of gap S1 has to be in the round's own area either way.
        let from_env = liveness_dir(Some("F:/round/artifact"));
        assert_eq!(
            from_env,
            std::path::Path::new("F:/round/artifact")
                .join("deterministic")
                .join("raw")
        );
        assert_eq!(
            liveness_dir(None),
            std::path::Path::new(".hoh/deterministic/raw")
        );
        assert_eq!(
            liveness_dir(Some("")),
            std::path::Path::new(".hoh/deterministic/raw"),
            "an empty variable is the same as an unset one"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn the_liveness_schedule_has_early_and_late_deadlines() {
        // Early deadlines land inside the short E3 window; the late ones are the
        // record gap S1 asks for, so a reader can cite "still advancing frames
        // long after the battery's last step".
        assert_eq!(next_deadline_ms(0), 2_000, "the first record is early");
        assert_eq!(next_deadline_ms(6), 60_000, "the last fixed one is late");
        assert_eq!(
            next_deadline_ms(7),
            60_000 + LIVENESS_INTERVAL_MS,
            "after the fixed schedule it keeps repeating"
        );
        assert!(
            next_deadline_ms(6) >= 30_000,
            "there is a deadline several seconds after the early window"
        );

        // How many deadlines have come due.
        assert_eq!(records_due(0, 0), 0, "nothing is due at launch");
        assert_eq!(records_due(0, 1_999), 0);
        assert_eq!(records_due(0, 2_000), 1, "the first deadline is 2 s");
        assert_eq!(records_due(0, 5_000), 2, "2 s and 4 s are both due");
        assert_eq!(records_due(0, 60_000), 7, "all fixed deadlines are due");
        assert_eq!(records_due(0, 90_000), 8, "then one per interval");
        assert_eq!(
            records_due(3, 0),
            3,
            "a suspended process never rewinds the count"
        );
        assert_eq!(
            records_due(0, u64::MAX),
            7 + (u64::MAX - 60_000) / LIVENESS_INTERVAL_MS,
            "a huge clock jump is computed in closed form, not by stepping"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_record_lands_in_the_jsonl_and_the_log_and_carries_the_game_frame() {
        // The persisted record the plan's gap S1 asks for: both files must be
        // appended (not truncated) and the JSON line must carry the *game's*
        // frame counter, so the record is a claim about the game and not about
        // the poller.
        let dir = std::env::temp_dir().join("hof_game_liveness_test");
        let _ = std::fs::remove_dir_all(&dir);
        let json = "{\"probe\":\"hof_game\",\"event\":\"liveness\",\"frame\":720}";
        let text = "hof_game liveness frame=720 coins=2 won=true x=210.00";
        persist_record(&dir, json, text);
        persist_record(&dir, json, text);

        let jsonl = std::fs::read_to_string(dir.join(LIVENESS_JSONL_NAME)).unwrap();
        let log = std::fs::read_to_string(dir.join(LIVENESS_LOG_NAME)).unwrap();
        assert_eq!(jsonl.lines().count(), 2, "the JSONL record must append");
        assert_eq!(log.lines().count(), 2, "the text log must append too");
        assert!(jsonl.contains("\"frame\":720"), "the game frame is the stamp");
        assert!(log.contains("frame=720"), "the game frame is the stamp");
        let _ = std::fs::remove_dir_all(&dir);
    }
}
HOH_END_WRITE_FILE

function.arguments:
{"command": "HOH_WRITE_FILE src/game.rs
//! The game's behaviour: a horizontal move, **two** coins, one goal, a jump,
//! the ground state that gives the jump a semantic basis (PRD §2 P1..P5), and
//! the game's **own persisted liveness record** (plan gap S1).
//!
//! The numbers below are the level's design, not the contract: a Developer is
//! expected to change them (and this file) as the round's increment.  Only the
//! type paths in [`crate::contract`] are frozen.
//!
//! Two properties are deliberate because the observation battery depends on
//! them:
//!
//! * **`move_dir` is a held level.**  The velocity is derived from it every
//!   frame, so writing `0` stops the player immediately and no inertia drifts
//!   (PRD P1).  Clearing it every frame is what SPIKE-1 measured as a 2 px move
//!   over 0.4 s.
//! * **The jump's edge is consumed by the game.**  `jump_pressed` is a level, so
//!   the game latches it: one press is one jump, and holding it does not bounce.
//!
//! ## The level (iteration 2: two coins, monotone counting — plan P2B)
//!
//! Two coins and a goal, all on the same ground plane, in increasing x.  Coin #1
//! and the goal keep iteration 1's positions, so the already-verified traces
//! stay the regression baseline; coin #2 is iteration 2's increment.
//!
//! The geometry is derived from iteration 1's own recorded drive of the
//! battery's session (`raw/e3_*.json`): the first held-right leg travels ~18
//! frames at ~3.4 px/frame and stops at x ~ 61, and the second leg passes
//! x ~ 100 by its first read.  Hence:
//!
//! ```text
//!   x = 0      player start
//!   x = 60     coin #1   reach 48..72: unchanged from iteration 1, so the first
//!                        leg definitely picks it up, while a read before any
//!                        input (x = 0) still sees `coins = 0`
//!   x = 90     coin #2   reach 78..102: the first leg stops ~17 px short of it,
//!                        so the read right after that leg is exactly 1; the
//!                        second leg walks straight through it (x ~ 78 within a
//!                        handful of frames), long before the goal, so the next
//!                        read is exactly 2
//!   x = 100    goal      unchanged from iteration 1, and past both coins, so the
//!                        win's false -> true happens *after* the second pickup,
//!                        at the very place iteration 1 already recorded
//! ```
//!
//! Walking right therefore takes the frozen `CoinCounter` 0 -> 1 -> 2 and only
//! then flips `WinFlag` false -> true, in that order, in one session — which is
//! exactly what this iteration's priority 3 re-proves in a single fresh headless
//! run.  The two reach windows are disjoint (48..72 and 78..102), which is what
//! keeps "1 after the first coin" and "2 after the second" separate readings
//! instead of one saturated number.
//!
//! A coin is a **real entity** with a `Transform`, and the pickup is a proximity
//! test against the player's x: touching a coin marks *that* coin collected and
//! increments the frozen counter once.  The per-coin `collected` flag is what
//! makes the count monotone: standing still on an already-collected coin, or
//! walking back past it, never counts it again.  The pickup radius is wider than
//! one frame's step at [`MAX_STEP_SECONDS`], so a slow frame cannot tunnel the
//! player past a coin.
//!
//! ## The win is on the walkable path (this iteration's priority 2)
//!
//! `GOAL_X` is past both coins on the *same* ground plane the player walks on,
//! and `detect_win` fires on `player_x >= goal_x` with the player standing
//! (`Grounded.on_ground` is true on the frame the flag flips, because a walk has
//! no airborne frames).  No new reflected type was added for the goal: the
//! frozen contract exposes no goal marker or goal x, so the acceptance gate
//! drops the "player x equals the goal's reachable x" equality sub-condition
//! rather than inventing a contract change.  What the game *can* do is keep the
//! goal unambiguously reachable by walking right, and both the unit tests below
//! and the battery's `e3_win_position` step assert that.
//!
//! ## The persisted liveness record (plan gap S1)
//!
//! `liveness_probe` appends one self-describing record per wall-clock deadline
//! (2 s, 4 s, 8 s, 15 s, 25 s, 40 s, 60 s after launch, then every 30 s) to
//! `.hoh/deterministic/raw/liveness.jsonl` **and** `…/liveness.log` under the
//! round's artifact directory ([`ARTIFACT_DIR_ENV`]), and echoes it on stderr.
//!
//! The record is stamped with the *game's own* [`FrameCounter`], never a poll
//! ordinal, and it carries the player transform, the ground state, the coin
//! counter and the win flag, so a reader can cite "this process was still
//! advancing frames `k` seconds after launch, with these values" long after the
//! short E3 battery window is over.  Wall-clock scheduling (not frame numbers)
//! is what makes a **late** record exist even if frames run far behind wall
//! time; the frame numbers in the record then prove the game really advanced.
//! Every failure mode is swallowed: a liveness probe must never be able to
//! stall, panic or fail the game it is proving alive.
//!
//! The frame order is `count_frames -> apply_input -> integrate ->
//! collect_coins -> detect_win -> liveness_probe`, so the whole of one frame's
//! motion happens before the coin/goal test and every value the game persists or
//! a tool can read is self-consistent at the end of the frame.
//!
//! The maths of one frame lives in small pure helpers (`plan_velocity`,
//! `step_motion`, `collects_at`, `win_reached`) that the systems are thin
//! wrappers around.  That is what makes the physics and the pickup testable
//! without a clock: the tests at the bottom of this file drive the very same
//! code the running game drives, with a fixed `dt` of one 60 Hz frame.

use bevy::prelude::*;

use crate::contract::{CoinCounter, FrameCounter, Grounded, InputIntent, Player, Velocity, WinFlag};

/// The ground plane's height.  Also the player's and every coin's height.
pub const GROUND_Y: f32 = -200.0;
/// Horizontal speed, in pixels per second.
pub const MOVE_SPEED: f32 = 200.0;
/// The upwards impulse, in pixels per second.
pub const JUMP_SPEED: f32 = 300.0;
/// Downwards acceleration, in pixels per second squared.
pub const GRAVITY: f32 = 1200.0;
/// The first coin's x.  Iteration 1's coin, unchanged; it is the first entry of
/// [`COIN_XS`], the authoritative list.
pub const COIN_X: f32 = 60.0;
/// The second coin's x: beyond the first, and clear of where the battery's first
/// held-right leg stops, so the counter has to go 0 -> 1 -> 2 over two legs
/// instead of saturating on the first pickup.
pub const COIN2_X: f32 = 90.0;
/// The coin positions on the x axis, in the order they are meant to be reached.
pub const COIN_XS: [f32; 2] = [COIN_X, COIN2_X];
/// How close the player's x has to be to a coin's x to pick it up.
///
/// At 60 Hz the player advances ~3.4 px per frame (at most one
/// [`MAX_STEP_SECONDS`] step, i.e. ~6.7 px), so a radius wider than one frame's
/// step is what makes the pickup impossible to skip over.  With this radius the
/// two coins' reach windows (48..72 and 78..102) are disjoint, which is what
/// keeps "1 after the first coin" and "2 after the second" separate readings.
pub const COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS: f32 = 12.0;
/// Where the goal sits on the x axis.  Iteration 1's position, unchanged: past
/// both coins on the same ground plane, so the coin count is already 2 when the
/// flag flips, and at the exact place whose win frame iteration 1 already proved
/// reachable.  It stays on the walkable path — no jump is needed to win.
pub const GOAL_X: f32 = 100.0;
/// The largest timestep one frame may integrate, so a slow first frame cannot
/// teleport the player past a coin or the goal.
pub const MAX_STEP_SECONDS: f32 = 1.0 / 30.0;

/// Wall-clock deadlines (milliseconds after launch) at which [`liveness_probe`]
/// persists a record.  The early ones land inside the short E3 battery window;
/// the later ones (`25 s`, `40 s`, `60 s`) are the *late* records gap S1 asks
/// for, and they exist no matter how the frame rate behaves, because the
/// schedule is wall time and not frame counts.
pub const LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS: [u64; 7] = [2_000, 4_000, 8_000, 15_000, 25_000, 40_000, 60_000];
/// Once the fixed deadlines are spent, records repeat at this interval, so a
/// long-lived session keeps being evidenced instead of going quiet after 60 s.
pub const LIVENESS_INTERVAL_MS: u64 = 30_000;
/// The evidence directory the records are persisted into, relative to the
/// working directory.
pub const LIVENESS_DIR_RELATIVE: &str = ".hoh/deterministic/raw";
/// The machine-readable record file (one JSON object per line).
pub const LIVENESS_JSONL_NAME: &str = "liveness.jsonl";
/// The human-readable record file (continues iteration 2's `liveness.log`).
pub const LIVENESS_LOG_NAME: &str = "liveness.log";

/// The launcher's environment variable naming the round's artifact directory.
///
/// When the game is launched with it set, the liveness records are written under
/// `<artifact>/deterministic/raw/` instead of relative to the working directory,
/// so the record lands in the round's own evidence area even if the process was
/// started from somewhere else.
pub const ARTIFACT_DIR_ENV: &str = "HOH_ARTIFACT_DIR";

/// The jump's own edge: `true` while `jump_pressed` is being held, so a level
/// that stays true is one jump and not a trampoline.
#[derive(Component, Debug, Default)]
struct JumpLatch(bool);

/// A pickable coin.  Private on purpose: the coin's *consequence* is the frozen
/// [`CoinCounter`], and nothing outside the game acts on a coin entity.
#[derive(Component, Debug, Clone, Copy)]
struct Coin {
    /// `true` once this coin has been picked up; a coin counts exactly once.
    collected: bool,
}

/// The goal post.  Also private: its consequence is the frozen [`WinFlag`].
#[derive(Component, Debug, Default)]
struct Goal;

/// The schedule of persisted liveness records (plan gap S1).
///
/// It is a *wall-clock* schedule: `started` is taken when the game boots and
/// every record is due at a fixed number of milliseconds after it, so the record
/// a reader cites for "still alive late in the round" exists regardless of how
/// many frames ran in between.  `written` is how many deadlines have been
/// served; [`next_deadline_ms`] turns it back into the next due time.
///
/// Nothing in the game derives behaviour from it — it only observes.
#[derive(Resource, Debug)]
struct LivenessClock {
    started: std::time::Instant,
    written: u64,
}

impl Default for LivenessClock {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self {
            started: std::time::Instant::now(),
            written: 0,
        }
    }
}

pub fn add(app: &mut App) {
    app.init_resource::<LivenessClock>();
    app.add_systems(Startup, (spawn_scene, announce_boot));
    app.add_systems(
        Update,
        (
            count_frames,
            apply_input,
            integrate,
            collect_coins,
            detect_win,
            liveness_probe,
        )
            .chain(),
    );
}

fn spawn_scene(mut commands: Commands) {
    // The player: the frozen marker, an engine `Transform`, the ground state and
    // the two game-owned pieces of state (`contract::Velocity` is registered so
    // the wire can localise a movement failure; `JumpLatch` stays private
    // because nothing outside the game can act on it).
    commands.spawn((
        Player,
        Transform::from_xyz(0.0, GROUND_Y, 0.0),
        Grounded::default(),
        Velocity::default(),
        JumpLatch::default(),
    ));

    // The coins: real entities, reachable on foot, each counted at most once.
    for coin_x in COIN_XS {
        commands.spawn((
            Coin { collected: false },
            Transform::from_xyz(coin_x, GROUND_Y, 0.0),
        ));
    }

    // The goal post, past both coins on the same ground plane.
    commands.spawn((Goal, Transform::from_xyz(GOAL_X, GROUND_Y, 0.0)));

    // The level's own tally: two coins to find.  `main` installed the contract
    // default (0 of 1) at startup; the level owns the real number, so the seed
    // is written here, next to the level that defines it.  `coins` itself still
    // starts at 0 (PRD P2).
    commands.insert_resource(CoinCounter {
        coins: 0,
        target: COIN_XS.len() as i64,
    });
}

/// The game's own clock (D297 (b)): every semantic tool reports this value as
/// `frame`, so it must advance exactly once per frame.
fn count_frames(mut counter: ResMut<FrameCounter>) {
    counter.frames = counter.frames.saturating_add(1);
}

/// Turn the held intent into velocity, and consume the jump's edge.
fn apply_input(
    intent: Res<InputIntent>,
    mut player: Query<(&mut Velocity, &mut Grounded, &mut JumpLatch), With<Player>>,
) {
    for (mut velocity, mut grounded, mut latch) in &mut player {
        plan_velocity(&mut velocity, &mut grounded, &mut latch, *intent);
    }
}

/// The input half of one frame, as a pure function.
///
/// `velocity.x` is **recomputed from the level** every frame, so writing
/// `move_dir = 0` stops the player on that frame and no inertia is carried over
/// (PRD P1).  The jump is an **edge** even though `jump_pressed` is a level: the
/// latch remembers that the press was already spent, so holding the key is one
/// jump and not a trampoline.
fn plan_velocity(
    velocity: &mut Velocity,
    grounded: &mut Grounded,
    latch: &mut JumpLatch,
    intent: InputIntent,
) {
    velocity.x = MOVE_SPEED * intent.move_dir as f32;
    if intent.jump_pressed && grounded.on_ground && !latch.0 {
        velocity.y = JUMP_SPEED;
        grounded.on_ground = false;
    }
    latch.0 = intent.jump_pressed;
}

/// Integrate one frame of motion and settle on the ground.
fn integrate(
    time: Res<Time>,
    mut player: Query<(&mut Transform, &mut Velocity, &mut Grounded), With<Player>>,
) {
    let dt = time.delta_secs().min(MAX_STEP_SECONDS);
    for (mut transform, mut velocity, mut grounded) in &mut player {
        step_motion(&mut transform, &mut velocity, &mut grounded, dt);
    }
}

/// The motion half of one frame, as a pure function.
///
/// Gravity is applied *before* the position update, and the ground is a clamp:
/// `y` can never go below [`GROUND_Y`], the downwards velocity is zeroed on
/// contact, and `Grounded.on_ground` is true exactly while standing.
fn step_motion(
    transform: &mut Transform,
    velocity: &mut Velocity,
    grounded: &mut Grounded,
    dt: f32,
) {
    transform.translation.x += velocity.x * dt;
    velocity.y -= GRAVITY * dt;
    transform.translation.y += velocity.y * dt;
    if transform.translation.y <= GROUND_Y {
        transform.translation.y = GROUND_Y;
        velocity.y = 0.0;
        grounded.on_ground = true;
    } else {
        grounded.on_ground = false;
    }
}

/// The pickup test, as a pure function: is the player within reach of a coin
/// that sits at `coin_x`?
fn collects_at(coin_x: f32, player_x: f32) -> bool {
    (player_x - coin_x).abs() <= COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS
}

/// The win test, as a pure function: has the player walked on to the goal?
fn win_reached(goal_x: f32, player_x: f32) -> bool {
    player_x >= goal_x
}

/// Touch a coin, incrementing the frozen counter once per coin (PRD P2).
///
/// The per-coin `collected` flag is the whole reason the counter is monotone:
/// the increment can only ever happen on the transition of one specific coin
/// from "untouched" to "touched", so standing on a coin, or walking back over
/// it, counts nothing.  That is iteration 2's plan requirement ("no re-trigger
/// when the player stands still on an already-collected coin").
fn collect_coins(
    mut counter: ResMut<CoinCounter>,
    player: Query<&Transform, With<Player>>,
    mut coins: Query<(&Transform, &mut Coin), Without<Player>>,
) {
    for player_transform in &player {
        let player_x = player_transform.translation.x;
        for (coin_transform, mut coin) in &mut coins {
            if !coin.collected && collects_at(coin_transform.translation.x, player_x) {
                coin.collected = true;
                counter.coins = counter.coins.saturating_add(1);
            }
        }
    }
}

/// Reaching the goal latches the win flag (PRD P3).
///
/// The write is **one-way**: `won` is only ever set, never cleared, so the
/// false -> true transition the criterion looks for can never be undone.  The
/// goal test is *independent* of the coins -- PRD P3 asks that reaching the goal
/// flips the flag, so a run that walks straight there must still win, and the
/// level simply places the goal past both coins so the natural walk collects
/// them on the way.
fn detect_win(
    mut win: ResMut<WinFlag>,
    player: Query<&Transform, With<Player>>,
    goal: Query<&Transform, With<Goal>>,
) {
    if win.won {
        return;
    }
    for player_transform in &player {
        let player_x = player_transform.translation.x;
        for goal_transform in &goal {
            if win_reached(goal_transform.translation.x, player_x) {
                win.won = true;
            }
        }
    }
}

/// The next wall-clock deadline (milliseconds after launch) that has not been
/// served yet, given `written` records already written.
///
/// The first [`LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS`] records use the fixed early schedule;
/// after that the interval is [`LIVENESS_INTERVAL_MS`].  Pure, so the schedule
/// is testable without waiting for it.
fn next_deadline_ms(written: u64) -> u64 {
    let fixed = LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS.len() as u64;
    if written < fixed {
        LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS[written as usize]
    } else {
        let last = LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS[LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS.len() - 1];
        last + LIVENESS_INTERVAL_MS * (written - fixed + 1)
    }
}

/// How many deadlines have come due by `elapsed_ms`, never fewer than `written`.
///
/// Computed in closed form rather than by stepping deadline by deadline, so a
/// process that was suspended for an hour (or a clock that jumped) cannot turn
/// this into a long loop.  Pure, so the catch-up rule is unit tested.
fn records_due(written: u64, elapsed_ms: u64) -> u64 {
    let fixed = LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS.len();
    let last = LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS[fixed - 1];
    let due = if elapsed_ms < last {
        LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS
            .iter()
            .filter(|deadline| **deadline <= elapsed_ms)
            .count() as u64
    } else {
        fixed as u64 + (elapsed_ms - last) / LIVENESS_INTERVAL_MS
    };
    due.max(written)
}

/// Resolve the liveness evidence directory, preferring [`ARTIFACT_DIR_ENV`] when
/// it is set.  Kept pure (the environment is passed in) so it is testable.
fn liveness_dir(artifact_dir: Option<&str>) -> std::path::PathBuf {
    let relative = std::path::Path::new(LIVENESS_DIR_RELATIVE);
    let tail = relative.strip_prefix(".hoh").unwrap_or(relative);
    match artifact_dir {
        Some(dir) if !dir.is_empty() => std::path::Path::new(dir).join(tail),
        _ => relative.to_path_buf(),
    }
}

/// The evidence directory for *this* process: [`liveness_dir`] fed from the
/// environment.
fn resolved_liveness_dir() -> std::path::PathBuf {
    liveness_dir(std::env::var(ARTIFACT_DIR_ENV).ok().as_deref())
}

/// Append one line to `path`, creating it if needed, swallowing every error.
fn append_line(path: &std::path::Path, line: &str) {
    use std::io::Write;
    if let Ok(mut file) = std::fs::OpenOptions::new()
        .create(true)
        .append(true)
        .open(path)
    {
        let _ = writeln!(file, "{line}");
    }
}

/// Persist one record: the JSON line into [`LIVENESS_JSONL_NAME`] and the
/// `key=value` line into [`LIVENESS_LOG_NAME`].
///
/// Both sinks are attempted independently and neither can fail the caller: a
/// missing directory, a full disk or a locked file is ignored, because a
/// liveness probe must never be able to stall or fail the game it is proving
/// alive.
fn persist_record(dir: &std::path::Path, json: &str, text: &str) {
    if std::fs::create_dir_all(dir).is_err() && !dir.is_dir() {
        return;
    }
    append_line(&dir.join(LIVENESS_JSONL_NAME), json);
    append_line(&dir.join(LIVENESS_LOG_NAME), text);
}

/// The wall-clock milliseconds of "now", or 0 if the system clock is before the
/// epoch (which cannot fail the game either).
fn wall_ms() -> u128 {
    std::time::SystemTime::now()
        .duration_since(std::time::UNIX_EPOCH)
        .map(|elapsed| elapsed.as_millis())
        .unwrap_or(0)
}

/// The one line that says the process booted, written before the first frame.
///
/// It carries the game's frame counter and the process id, so the `raw/`
/// evidence area starts with a record proving *this* process reached startup.
fn announce_boot(frames: Res<FrameCounter>) {
    let pid = std::process::id();
    let wall = wall_ms();
    let json = format!(
        "{{\"probe\":\"hof_game\",\"event\":\"boot\",\"frame\":{},\"pid\":{},\"wall_ms\":{}}}",
        frames.frames, pid, wall
    );
    let text = format!(
        "hof_game boot frame={} pid={} wall_ms={}",
        frames.frames, pid, wall
    );
    eprintln!("{text}");
    persist_record(&resolved_liveness_dir(), &json, &text);
}

/// The persisted liveness record (plan gap S1).
///
/// Due on a **wall-clock** schedule ([`next_deadline_ms`]), so a record exists
/// seconds after launch whatever the frame rate does, and stamped with the
/// game's own [`FrameCounter`], so a reader can prove the game advanced rather
/// than the poller repeating itself.  It also carries the player transform, the
/// ground state, the coin counter and the win flag, which is what makes a late
/// record citable as "alive *and* still in the state the battery left it in".
///
/// Every failure mode is swallowed on purpose.  It observes; it never changes
/// behaviour.
fn liveness_probe(
    frames: Res<FrameCounter>,
    coins: Res<CoinCounter>,
    win: Res<WinFlag>,
    player: Query<(&Transform, &Grounded), With<Player>>,
    mut clock: ResMut<LivenessClock>,
) {
    let elapsed_ms = clock.started.elapsed().as_millis().min(u64::MAX as u128) as u64;
    let due = records_due(clock.written, elapsed_ms);
    if due <= clock.written {
        return;
    }
    // A process that was suspended for a long time has several deadlines behind
    // it; one record catches the schedule up (the values would be identical
    // anyway) instead of writing a burst of stale lines.
    clock.written = due;

    let pid = std::process::id();
    let wall = wall_ms();
    let frame = frames.frames;
    let player_state = player
        .iter()
        .next()
        .map(|(transform, grounded)| {
            (
                transform.translation.x,
                transform.translation.y,
                grounded.on_ground,
            )
        })
        .unwrap_or((f32::NAN, f32::NAN, false));
    let (x, y, on_ground) = player_state;

    let json = format!(
        "{{\"probe\":\"hof_game\",\"event\":\"liveness\",\"frame\":{frame},\"pid\":{pid},\
         \"wall_ms\":{wall},\"elapsed_ms\":{elapsed_ms},\"coins\":{},\"target\":{},\
         \"won\":{},\"x\":{x:.2},\"y\":{y:.2},\"on_ground\":{on_ground}}}",
        coins.coins, coins.target, win.won
    );
    let text = format!(
        "hof_game liveness frame={frame} pid={pid} wall_ms={wall} elapsed_ms={elapsed_ms} \
         coins={} target={} won={} x={x:.2} y={y:.2} on_ground={on_ground}",
        coins.coins, coins.target, win.won
    );
    eprintln!("{text}");
    persist_record(&resolved_liveness_dir(), &json, &text);
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    /// One 60 Hz game frame, the rate `main`'s schedule runner is set to.
    const DT: f32 = 1.0 / 60.0;

    /// The level from [`spawn_scene`], minus the ECS: the tests drive the very
    /// same helpers (`plan_velocity`, `step_motion`, `collects_at`,
    /// `win_reached`) the running systems call, so a behaviour that passes here
    /// is the behaviour the game has, not a copy of it.
    struct Sim {
        transform: Transform,
        velocity: Velocity,
        grounded: Grounded,
        latch: JumpLatch,
        coins: Vec<(f32, bool)>,
        counter: CoinCounter,
        win: WinFlag,
    }

    impl Sim {
        fn new() -> Self {
            Self {
                transform: Transform::from_xyz(0.0, GROUND_Y, 0.0),
                velocity: Velocity::default(),
                grounded: Grounded::default(),
                latch: JumpLatch::default(),
                coins: COIN_XS.iter().map(|x| (*x, false)).collect(),
                counter: CoinCounter {
                    coins: 0,
                    target: COIN_XS.len() as i64,
                },
                win: WinFlag::default(),
            }
        }

        /// One frame of the same pipeline the running game runs, in the same
        /// order: input, motion, pickup, goal.
        fn frame(&mut self, intent: InputIntent) {
            plan_velocity(&mut self.velocity, &mut self.grounded, &mut self.latch, intent);
            step_motion(&mut self.transform, &mut self.velocity, &mut self.grounded, DT);
            self.collect();
            self.reach_goal();
        }

        /// The pickup half of one frame, exactly like `collect_coins`.
        fn collect(&mut self) {
            let player_x = self.transform.translation.x;
            for (coin_x, collected) in &mut self.coins {
                if !*collected && collects_at(*coin_x, player_x) {
                    *collected = true;
                    self.counter.coins = self.counter.coins.saturating_add(1);
                }
            }
        }

        /// The goal half of one frame, exactly like `detect_win` (one-way).
        fn reach_goal(&mut self) {
            if !self.win.won && win_reached(GOAL_X, self.transform.translation.x) {
                self.win.won = true;
            }
        }
    }

    fn right() -> InputIntent {
        InputIntent {
            move_dir: 1,
            jump_pressed: false,
        }
    }

    fn left() -> InputIntent {
        InputIntent {
            move_dir: -1,
            jump_pressed: false,
        }
    }

    fn stop() -> InputIntent {
        InputIntent {
            move_dir: 0,
            jump_pressed: false,
        }
    }

    fn hold_jump() -> InputIntent {
        InputIntent {
            move_dir: 0,
            jump_pressed: true,
        }
    }

    #[test]
    fn held_input_moves_and_release_stops_without_inertia() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        for _ in 0..30 {
            sim.frame(right());
        }
        let moved = sim.transform.translation.x;
        assert!(
            moved > 90.0 && moved < 110.0,
            "30 frames at 200 px/s is 100 px, got {moved}"
        );

        // Release: level 0 must zero the velocity on the very first frame.
        for _ in 0..30 {
            sim.frame(stop());
        }
        assert_eq!(
            sim.transform.translation.x, moved,
            "released input must freeze x"
        );
        assert_eq!(sim.velocity.x, 0.0);
    }

    #[test]
    fn jump_rises_then_falls_and_lands() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        assert!(sim.grounded.on_ground, "the player stands before the press");

        let take_off_y = sim.transform.translation.y;

        // The very frame the press arrives: ground state off, rising.
        plan_velocity(
            &mut sim.velocity,
            &mut sim.grounded,
            &mut sim.latch,
            hold_jump(),
        );
        assert!(!sim.grounded.on_ground, "the press must clear the ground state");
        step_motion(&mut sim.transform, &mut sim.velocity, &mut sim.grounded, DT);
        assert!(
            sim.transform.translation.y > take_off_y,
            "the first airborne frame must already be higher"
        );

        // Keep holding: the latch means no second jump, and the player tracks a
        // full arc back to the ground.
        let mut trace = vec![sim.transform.translation.y];
        for _ in 0..60 {
            sim.frame(hold_jump());
            trace.push(sim.transform.translation.y);
        }

        let apex = trace.iter().cloned().fold(f32::MIN, f32::max);
        assert!(apex > take_off_y + 30.0, "the jump must rise: apex {apex}");
        let apex_at = trace.iter().position(|y| *y == apex).unwrap();
        assert!(apex_at > 0, "the apex is not the first frame");
        assert!(
            trace[apex_at..].iter().any(|y| *y < apex - 5.0),
            "the jump must come back down"
        );
        assert!(
            (trace[trace.len() - 1] - take_off_y).abs() < f32::EPSILON,
            "the jump must land back on the ground"
        );
        assert!(sim.grounded.on_ground, "and the ground state must be true again");
    }

    #[test]
    fn grounded_is_false_for_the_airborne_frames() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        // Rise phase: the first ~10 frames are still going up, so the player is
        // off the ground for all of them.
        sim.frame(hold_jump());
        for _ in 0..8 {
            assert!(
                !sim.grounded.on_ground,
                "the player is airborne while rising, y = {}",
                sim.transform.translation.y
            );
            assert!(sim.transform.translation.y > GROUND_Y);
            sim.frame(hold_jump());
        }
    }

    #[test]
    fn held_jump_is_one_jump_not_a_trampoline() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        let mut take_offs = 0;
        for _ in 0..120 {
            let was_grounded = sim.grounded.on_ground;
            plan_velocity(
                &mut sim.velocity,
                &mut sim.grounded,
                &mut sim.latch,
                hold_jump(),
            );
            if was_grounded && !sim.grounded.on_ground {
                take_offs += 1;
            }
            step_motion(&mut sim.transform, &mut sim.velocity, &mut sim.grounded, DT);
        }
        assert_eq!(take_offs, 1, "a held press is exactly one jump");
    }

    #[test]
    fn walking_right_collects_both_coins_monotonically() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 0, "the run starts at 0 coins");
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.target, 2, "the level holds two coins");

        let mut trace = Vec::new();
        let mut first_coin_at = None;
        let mut second_coin_at = None;
        for frame_index in 0..120 {
            sim.frame(right());
            trace.push(sim.counter.coins);
            if first_coin_at.is_none() && sim.counter.coins >= 1 {
                first_coin_at = Some(frame_index);
                assert!(sim.transform.translation.x >= COIN_X - COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS);
            }
            if second_coin_at.is_none() && sim.counter.coins >= 2 {
                second_coin_at = Some(frame_index);
                assert!(sim.transform.translation.x >= COIN2_X - COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS);
            }
        }

        let first = first_coin_at.expect("coin #1 is reachable on foot");
        let second = second_coin_at.expect("coin #2 is reachable on foot");
        assert!(first < second, "the coins are reached in their x order");
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 2, "two coins, counted twice");

        // Monotone: the sequence never decreases and steps one at a time.
        for pair in trace.windows(2) {
            assert!(pair[1] >= pair[0], "the count must never decrease: {trace:?}");
            assert!(pair[1] - pair[0] <= 1, "at most one coin per frame: {trace:?}");
        }

        // Walking back left never clears either value.
        for _ in 0..120 {
            sim.frame(left());
        }
        assert!(sim.transform.translation.x < COIN_X, "the player walked back");
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 2, "the coin counter never decreases");
    }

    #[test]
    fn the_first_leg_reads_one_and_the_second_leg_reads_two() {
        // The shape of the battery's drive: hold right, stop and read, hold
        // right again, read.  The stop lands inside coin #1's reach and clear of
        // coin #2's, so the read between the legs is exactly 1, and the second
        // leg walks through coin #2, making the next read 2.
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        for _ in 0..18 {
            sim.frame(right());
        }
        sim.frame(stop());
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 1, "the read after the first leg is 1");
        assert!(
            sim.transform.translation.x < COIN2_X - COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS,
            "x = {} must be short of coin #2's reach",
            sim.transform.translation.x
        );
        assert!(
            sim.transform.translation.x >= COIN_X - COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS,
            "x = {} must be inside coin #1's reach",
            sim.transform.translation.x
        );

        // The battery's idle wait must not change it.
        for _ in 0..10 {
            sim.frame(stop());
        }
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 1, "standing still must not re-count");

        for _ in 0..15 {
            sim.frame(right());
        }
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 2, "the read after the second leg is 2");
        assert!(sim.win.won, "and the same leg walks on to the goal");
        assert!(sim.grounded.on_ground, "reached standing on the ground");
    }

    #[test]
    fn one_walk_right_runs_the_whole_loop_in_one_session() {
        // This iteration's priority 3, as a test: a *single* continuous walk
        // right must take `coins` 0 -> 1 -> 2 and then flip `won` false -> true,
        // in that order, without a jump and without leaving the ground.  That is
        // what rules out "green while coins read 0".
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 0, "the run starts at 0 coins");
        assert!(!sim.win.won, "the run starts without the win");

        let mut first_pickup = None;
        let mut second_pickup = None;
        let mut won_at = None;
        for frame_index in 0..180 {
            sim.frame(right());
            if first_pickup.is_none() && sim.counter.coins == 1 {
                first_pickup = Some(frame_index);
            }
            if second_pickup.is_none() && sim.counter.coins == 2 {
                second_pickup = Some(frame_index);
            }
            if won_at.is_none() && sim.win.won {
                won_at = Some(frame_index);
            }
        }

        let first = first_pickup.expect("the walk reaches coin #1");
        let second = second_pickup.expect("the walk reaches coin #2");
        let win = won_at.expect("the walk reaches the goal");
        assert!(first < second, "coin #1 comes first");
        assert!(
            second <= win,
            "the win cannot be reached before the second coin: {second} > {win}"
        );
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 2, "both coins are counted");
        assert!(sim.win.won, "and the flag is true at the end of the walk");
        assert!(
            sim.transform.translation.x >= GOAL_X,
            "the player is at the goal: x = {}",
            sim.transform.translation.x
        );
        assert!(
            sim.grounded.on_ground,
            "the win happens standing on the walkable ground, no jump needed"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn standing_still_on_a_collected_coin_does_not_retrigger_it() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        // Walk on to the first coin and stop exactly on it.
        while sim.transform.translation.x < COIN_X {
            sim.frame(right());
        }
        sim.frame(stop());
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 1, "the first coin is counted once");

        // One second of standing exactly on the coin with the input released.
        for _ in 0..60 {
            sim.frame(stop());
        }
        assert_eq!(
            sim.counter.coins, 1,
            "standing still on a collected coin must not count it again"
        );
        assert!(
            collects_at(COIN_X, sim.transform.translation.x),
            "the player is still within reach of coin #1"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn the_goal_is_past_both_coins_and_the_win_is_one_way() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        assert!(!sim.win.won, "the run starts without the win");
        assert!(GOAL_X > COIN2_X, "the goal is past both coins");

        let mut won_at = None;
        for frame_index in 0..120 {
            sim.frame(right());
            if won_at.is_none() && sim.win.won {
                won_at = Some(frame_index);
                assert_eq!(
                    sim.counter.coins, 2,
                    "the win is only reachable after both coins"
                );
                assert!(sim.transform.translation.x >= GOAL_X);
            }
        }
        assert!(won_at.is_some(), "walking right must reach the goal");
        assert!(sim.grounded.on_ground, "and reach it standing on the ground");

        // One-way: walking back left never clears the flag.
        for _ in 0..120 {
            sim.frame(left());
        }
        assert!(sim.transform.translation.x < COIN_X, "the player did walk back");
        assert!(sim.win.won, "the win flag is one-way");
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 2, "and the count stays put");
    }

    #[test]
    fn nothing_is_collected_at_the_starting_position() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        assert!(!collects_at(COIN_X, 0.0), "the start is clear of coin #1");
        assert!(!collects_at(COIN2_X, 0.0), "the start is clear of coin #2");
        assert!(!win_reached(GOAL_X, 0.0), "the start is short of the goal");
        sim.frame(stop());
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 0, "the run starts at 0 coins");
        assert!(!sim.win.won, "the run starts without the win");
    }

    #[test]
    fn the_two_coin_windows_are_disjoint_and_ordered() {
        // The whole point of the geometry: coin #1's reach window ends before
        // coin #2's begins, so no single position collects both at once and the
        // counter has to step 0 -> 1 -> 2.
        let coin1_far = COIN_X + COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS;
        let coin2_near = COIN2_X - COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS;
        assert!(
            coin1_far < coin2_near,
            "coin #1 reach ends at {coin1_far}, coin #2 begins at {coin2_near}"
        );
        assert!(COIN2_X > COIN_X, "the coins are ordered left to right");
        assert!(GOAL_X > COIN2_X, "the goal is beyond coin #2");
        assert!(
            COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS > MOVE_SPEED * MAX_STEP_SECONDS,
            "one frame's step must be smaller than the reach window"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn the_liveness_dir_prefers_the_rounds_artifact_dir() {
        // The launcher exports HOH_ARTIFACT_DIR; when it is there the records
        // must land under that directory's `deterministic/raw/`, and when it is
        // not the working-directory-relative path is used.  The liveness
        // evidence of gap S1 has to be in the round's own area either way.
        let from_env = liveness_dir(Some("F:/round/artifact"));
        assert_eq!(
            from_env,
            std::path::Path::new("F:/round/artifact")
                .join("deterministic")
                .join("raw")
        );
        assert_eq!(
            liveness_dir(None),
            std::path::Path::new(".hoh/deterministic/raw")
        );
        assert_eq!(
            liveness_dir(Some("")),
            std::path::Path::new(".hoh/deterministic/raw"),
            "an empty variable is the same as an unset one"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn the_liveness_schedule_has_early_and_late_deadlines() {
        // Early deadlines land inside the short E3 window; the late ones are the
        // record gap S1 asks for, so a reader can cite "still advancing frames
        // long after the battery's last step".
        assert_eq!(next_deadline_ms(0), 2_000, "the first record is early");
        assert_eq!(next_deadline_ms(6), 60_000, "the last fixed one is late");
        assert_eq!(
            next_deadline_ms(7),
            60_000 + LIVENESS_INTERVAL_MS,
            "after the fixed schedule it keeps repeating"
        );
        assert!(
            next_deadline_ms(6) >= 30_000,
            "there is a deadline several seconds after the early window"
        );

        // How many deadlines have come due.
        assert_eq!(records_due(0, 0), 0, "nothing is due at launch");
        assert_eq!(records_due(0, 1_999), 0);
        assert_eq!(records_due(0, 2_000), 1, "the first deadline is 2 s");
        assert_eq!(records_due(0, 5_000), 2, "2 s and 4 s are both due");
        assert_eq!(records_due(0, 60_000), 7, "all fixed deadlines are due");
        assert_eq!(records_due(0, 90_000), 8, "then one per interval");
        assert_eq!(
            records_due(3, 0),
            3,
            "a suspended process never rewinds the count"
        );
        assert_eq!(
            records_due(0, u64::MAX),
            7 + (u64::MAX - 60_000) / LIVENESS_INTERVAL_MS,
            "a huge clock jump is computed in closed form, not by stepping"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_record_lands_in_the_jsonl_and_the_log_and_carries_the_game_frame() {
        // The persisted record the plan's gap S1 asks for: both files must be
        // appended (not truncated) and the JSON line must carry the *game's*
        // frame counter, so the record is a claim about the game and not about
        // the poller.
        let dir = std::env::temp_dir().join("hof_game_liveness_test");
        let _ = std::fs::remove_dir_all(&dir);
        let json = "{\"probe\":\"hof_game\",\"event\":\"liveness\",\"frame\":720}";
        let text = "hof_game liveness frame=720 coins=2 won=true x=210.00";
        persist_record(&dir, json, text);
        persist_record(&dir, json, text);

        let jsonl = std::fs::read_to_string(dir.join(LIVENESS_JSONL_NAME)).unwrap();
        let log = std::fs::read_to_string(dir.join(LIVENESS_LOG_NAME)).unwrap();
        assert_eq!(jsonl.lines().count(), 2, "the JSONL record must append");
        assert_eq!(log.lines().count(), 2, "the text log must append too");
        assert!(jsonl.contains("\"frame\":720"), "the game frame is the stamp");
        assert!(log.contains("frame=720"), "the game frame is the stamp");
        let _ = std::fs::remove_dir_all(&dir);
    }
}
HOH_END_WRITE_FILE"}

{"command": "HOH_WRITE_FILE src/game.rs
//! The game's behaviour: a horizontal move, **two** coins, one goal, a jump,
//! the ground state that gives the jump a semantic basis (PRD §2 P1..P5), and
//! the game's **own persisted liveness record** (plan gap S1).
//!
//! The numbers below are the level's design, not the contract: a Developer is
//! expected to change them (and this file) as the round's increment.  Only the
//! type paths in [`crate::contract`] are frozen.
//!
//! Two properties are deliberate because the observation battery depends on
//! them:
//!
//! * **`move_dir` is a held level.**  The velocity is derived from it every
//!   frame, so writing `0` stops the player immediately and no inertia drifts
//!   (PRD P1).  Clearing it every frame is what SPIKE-1 measured as a 2 px move
//!   over 0.4 s.
//! * **The jump's edge is consumed by the game.**  `jump_pressed` is a level, so
//!   the game latches it: one press is one jump, and holding it does not bounce.
//!
//! ## The level (iteration 2: two coins, monotone counting — plan P2B)
//!
//! Two coins and a goal, all on the same ground plane, in increasing x.  Coin #1
//! and the goal keep iteration 1's positions, so the already-verified traces
//! stay the regression baseline; coin #2 is iteration 2's increment.
//!
//! The geometry is derived from iteration 1's own recorded drive of the
//! battery's session (`raw/e3_*.json`): the first held-right leg travels ~18
//! frames at ~3.4 px/frame and stops at x ~ 61, and the second leg passes
//! x ~ 100 by its first read.  Hence:
//!
//! ```text
//!   x = 0      player start
//!   x = 60     coin #1   reach 48..72: unchanged from iteration 1, so the first
//!                        leg definitely picks it up, while a read before any
//!                        input (x = 0) still sees `coins = 0`
//!   x = 90     coin #2   reach 78..102: the first leg stops ~17 px short of it,
//!                        so the read right after that leg is exactly 1; the
//!                        second leg walks straight through it (x ~ 78 within a
//!                        handful of frames), long before the goal, so the next
//!                        read is exactly 2
//!   x = 100    goal      unchanged from iteration 1, and past both coins, so the
//!                        win's false -> true happens *after* the second pickup,
//!                        at the very place iteration 1 already recorded
//! ```
//!
//! Walking right therefore takes the frozen `CoinCounter` 0 -> 1 -> 2 and only
//! then flips `WinFlag` false -> true, in that order, in one session — which is
//! exactly what this iteration's priority 3 re-proves in a single fresh headless
//! run.  The two reach windows are disjoint (48..72 and 78..102), which is what
//! keeps "1 after the first coin" and "2 after the second" separate readings
//! instead of one saturated number.
//!
//! A coin is a **real entity** with a `Transform`, and the pickup is a proximity
//! test against the player's x: touching a coin marks *that* coin collected and
//! increments the frozen counter once.  The per-coin `collected` flag is what
//! makes the count monotone: standing still on an already-collected coin, or
//! walking back past it, never counts it again.  The pickup radius is wider than
//! one frame's step at [`MAX_STEP_SECONDS`], so a slow frame cannot tunnel the
//! player past a coin.
//!
//! ## The win is on the walkable path (this iteration's priority 2)
//!
//! `GOAL_X` is past both coins on the *same* ground plane the player walks on,
//! and `detect_win` fires on `player_x >= goal_x` with the player standing
//! (`Grounded.on_ground` is true on the frame the flag flips, because a walk has
//! no airborne frames).  No new reflected type was added for the goal: the
//! frozen contract exposes no goal marker or goal x, so the acceptance gate
//! drops the "player x equals the goal's reachable x" equality sub-condition
//! rather than inventing a contract change.  What the game *can* do is keep the
//! goal unambiguously reachable by walking right, and both the unit tests below
//! and the battery's `e3_win_position` step assert that.
//!
//! ## The persisted liveness record (plan gap S1)
//!
//! `liveness_probe` appends one self-describing record per wall-clock deadline
//! (2 s, 4 s, 8 s, 15 s, 25 s, 40 s, 60 s after launch, then every 30 s) to
//! `.hoh/deterministic/raw/liveness.jsonl` **and** `…/liveness.log` under the
//! round's artifact directory ([`ARTIFACT_DIR_ENV`]), and echoes it on stderr.
//!
//! The record is stamped with the *game's own* [`FrameCounter`], never a poll
//! ordinal, and it carries the player transform, the ground state, the coin
//! counter and the win flag, so a reader can cite "this process was still
//! advancing frames `k` seconds after launch, with these values" long after the
//! short E3 battery window is over.  Wall-clock scheduling (not frame numbers)
//! is what makes a **late** record exist even if frames run far behind wall
//! time; the frame numbers in the record then prove the game really advanced.
//! Every failure mode is swallowed: a liveness probe must never be able to
//! stall, panic or fail the game it is proving alive.
//!
//! The frame order is `count_frames -> apply_input -> integrate ->
//! collect_coins -> detect_win -> liveness_probe`, so the whole of one frame's
//! motion happens before the coin/goal test and every value the game persists or
//! a tool can read is self-consistent at the end of the frame.
//!
//! The maths of one frame lives in small pure helpers (`plan_velocity`,
//! `step_motion`, `collects_at`, `win_reached`) that the systems are thin
//! wrappers around.  That is what makes the physics and the pickup testable
//! without a clock: the tests at the bottom of this file drive the very same
//! code the running game drives, with a fixed `dt` of one 60 Hz frame.

use bevy::prelude::*;

use crate::contract::{CoinCounter, FrameCounter, Grounded, InputIntent, Player, Velocity, WinFlag};

/// The ground plane's height.  Also the player's and every coin's height.
pub const GROUND_Y: f32 = -200.0;
/// Horizontal speed, in pixels per second.
pub const MOVE_SPEED: f32 = 200.0;
/// The upwards impulse, in pixels per second.
pub const JUMP_SPEED: f32 = 300.0;
/// Downwards acceleration, in pixels per second squared.
pub const GRAVITY: f32 = 1200.0;
/// The first coin's x.  Iteration 1's coin, unchanged; it is the first entry of
/// [`COIN_XS`], the authoritative list.
pub const COIN_X: f32 = 60.0;
/// The second coin's x: beyond the first, and clear of where the battery's first
/// held-right leg stops, so the counter has to go 0 -> 1 -> 2 over two legs
/// instead of saturating on the first pickup.
pub const COIN2_X: f32 = 90.0;
/// The coin positions on the x axis, in the order they are meant to be reached.
pub const COIN_XS: [f32; 2] = [COIN_X, COIN2_X];
/// How close the player's x has to be to a coin's x to pick it up.
///
/// At 60 Hz the player advances ~3.4 px per frame (at most one
/// [`MAX_STEP_SECONDS`] step, i.e. ~6.7 px), so a radius wider than one frame's
/// step is what makes the pickup impossible to skip over.  With this radius the
/// two coins' reach windows (48..72 and 78..102) are disjoint, which is what
/// keeps "1 after the first coin" and "2 after the second" separate readings.
pub const COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS: f32 = 12.0;
/// Where the goal sits on the x axis.  Iteration 1's position, unchanged: past
/// both coins on the same ground plane, so the coin count is already 2 when the
/// flag flips, and at the exact place whose win frame iteration 1 already proved
/// reachable.  It stays on the walkable path — no jump is needed to win.
pub const GOAL_X: f32 = 100.0;
/// The largest timestep one frame may integrate, so a slow first frame cannot
/// teleport the player past a coin or the goal.
pub const MAX_STEP_SECONDS: f32 = 1.0 / 30.0;

/// Wall-clock deadlines (milliseconds after launch) at which [`liveness_probe`]
/// persists a record.  The early ones land inside the short E3 battery window;
/// the later ones (`25 s`, `40 s`, `60 s`) are the *late* records gap S1 asks
/// for, and they exist no matter how the frame rate behaves, because the
/// schedule is wall time and not frame counts.
pub const LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS: [u64; 7] = [2_000, 4_000, 8_000, 15_000, 25_000, 40_000, 60_000];
/// Once the fixed deadlines are spent, records repeat at this interval, so a
/// long-lived session keeps being evidenced instead of going quiet after 60 s.
pub const LIVENESS_INTERVAL_MS: u64 = 30_000;
/// The evidence directory the records are persisted into, relative to the
/// working directory.
pub const LIVENESS_DIR_RELATIVE: &str = ".hoh/deterministic/raw";
/// The machine-readable record file (one JSON object per line).
pub const LIVENESS_JSONL_NAME: &str = "liveness.jsonl";
/// The human-readable record file (continues iteration 2's `liveness.log`).
pub const LIVENESS_LOG_NAME: &str = "liveness.log";

/// The launcher's environment variable naming the round's artifact directory.
///
/// When the game is launched with it set, the liveness records are written under
/// `<artifact>/deterministic/raw/` instead of relative to the working directory,
/// so the record lands in the round's own evidence area even if the process was
/// started from somewhere else.
pub const ARTIFACT_DIR_ENV: &str = "HOH_ARTIFACT_DIR";

/// The jump's own edge: `true` while `jump_pressed` is being held, so a level
/// that stays true is one jump and not a trampoline.
#[derive(Component, Debug, Default)]
struct JumpLatch(bool);

/// A pickable coin.  Private on purpose: the coin's *consequence* is the frozen
/// [`CoinCounter`], and nothing outside the game acts on a coin entity.
#[derive(Component, Debug, Clone, Copy)]
struct Coin {
    /// `true` once this coin has been picked up; a coin counts exactly once.
    collected: bool,
}

/// The goal post.  Also private: its consequence is the frozen [`WinFlag`].
#[derive(Component, Debug, Default)]
struct Goal;

/// The schedule of persisted liveness records (plan gap S1).
///
/// It is a *wall-clock* schedule: `started` is taken when the game boots and
/// every record is due at a fixed number of milliseconds after it, so the record
/// a reader cites for "still alive late in the round" exists regardless of how
/// many frames ran in between.  `written` is how many deadlines have been
/// served; [`next_deadline_ms`] turns it back into the next due time.
///
/// Nothing in the game derives behaviour from it — it only observes.
#[derive(Resource, Debug)]
struct LivenessClock {
    started: std::time::Instant,
    written: u64,
}

impl Default for LivenessClock {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self {
            started: std::time::Instant::now(),
            written: 0,
        }
    }
}

pub fn add(app: &mut App) {
    app.init_resource::<LivenessClock>();
    app.add_systems(Startup, (spawn_scene, announce_boot));
    app.add_systems(
        Update,
        (
            count_frames,
            apply_input,
            integrate,
            collect_coins,
            detect_win,
            liveness_probe,
        )
            .chain(),
    );
}

fn spawn_scene(mut commands: Commands) {
    // The player: the frozen marker, an engine `Transform`, the ground state and
    // the two game-owned pieces of state (`contract::Velocity` is registered so
    // the wire can localise a movement failure; `JumpLatch` stays private
    // because nothing outside the game can act on it).
    commands.spawn((
        Player,
        Transform::from_xyz(0.0, GROUND_Y, 0.0),
        Grounded::default(),
        Velocity::default(),
        JumpLatch::default(),
    ));

    // The coins: real entities, reachable on foot, each counted at most once.
    for coin_x in COIN_XS {
        commands.spawn((
            Coin { collected: false },
            Transform::from_xyz(coin_x, GROUND_Y, 0.0),
        ));
    }

    // The goal post, past both coins on the same ground plane.
    commands.spawn((Goal, Transform::from_xyz(GOAL_X, GROUND_Y, 0.0)));

    // The level's own tally: two coins to find.  `main` installed the contract
    // default (0 of 1) at startup; the level owns the real number, so the seed
    // is written here, next to the level that defines it.  `coins` itself still
    // starts at 0 (PRD P2).
    commands.insert_resource(CoinCounter {
        coins: 0,
        target: COIN_XS.len() as i64,
    });
}

/// The game's own clock (D297 (b)): every semantic tool reports this value as
/// `frame`, so it must advance exactly once per frame.
fn count_frames(mut counter: ResMut<FrameCounter>) {
    counter.frames = counter.frames.saturating_add(1);
}

/// Turn the held intent into velocity, and consume the jump's edge.
fn apply_input(
    intent: Res<InputIntent>,
    mut player: Query<(&mut Velocity, &mut Grounded, &mut JumpLatch), With<Player>>,
) {
    for (mut velocity, mut grounded, mut latch) in &mut player {
        plan_velocity(&mut velocity, &mut grounded, &mut latch, *intent);
    }
}

/// The input half of one frame, as a pure function.
///
/// `velocity.x` is **recomputed from the level** every frame, so writing
/// `move_dir = 0` stops the player on that frame and no inertia is carried over
/// (PRD P1).  The jump is an **edge** even though `jump_pressed` is a level: the
/// latch remembers that the press was already spent, so holding the key is one
/// jump and not a trampoline.
fn plan_velocity(
    velocity: &mut Velocity,
    grounded: &mut Grounded,
    latch: &mut JumpLatch,
    intent: InputIntent,
) {
    velocity.x = MOVE_SPEED * intent.move_dir as f32;
    if intent.jump_pressed && grounded.on_ground && !latch.0 {
        velocity.y = JUMP_SPEED;
        grounded.on_ground = false;
    }
    latch.0 = intent.jump_pressed;
}

/// Integrate one frame of motion and settle on the ground.
fn integrate(
    time: Res<Time>,
    mut player: Query<(&mut Transform, &mut Velocity, &mut Grounded), With<Player>>,
) {
    let dt = time.delta_secs().min(MAX_STEP_SECONDS);
    for (mut transform, mut velocity, mut grounded) in &mut player {
        step_motion(&mut transform, &mut velocity, &mut grounded, dt);
    }
}

/// The motion half of one frame, as a pure function.
///
/// Gravity is applied *before* the position update, and the ground is a clamp:
/// `y` can never go below [`GROUND_Y`], the downwards velocity is zeroed on
/// contact, and `Grounded.on_ground` is true exactly while standing.
fn step_motion(
    transform: &mut Transform,
    velocity: &mut Velocity,
    grounded: &mut Grounded,
    dt: f32,
) {
    transform.translation.x += velocity.x * dt;
    velocity.y -= GRAVITY * dt;
    transform.translation.y += velocity.y * dt;
    if transform.translation.y <= GROUND_Y {
        transform.translation.y = GROUND_Y;
        velocity.y = 0.0;
        grounded.on_ground = true;
    } else {
        grounded.on_ground = false;
    }
}

/// The pickup test, as a pure function: is the player within reach of a coin
/// that sits at `coin_x`?
fn collects_at(coin_x: f32, player_x: f32) -> bool {
    (player_x - coin_x).abs() <= COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS
}

/// The win test, as a pure function: has the player walked on to the goal?
fn win_reached(goal_x: f32, player_x: f32) -> bool {
    player_x >= goal_x
}

/// Touch a coin, incrementing the frozen counter once per coin (PRD P2).
///
/// The per-coin `collected` flag is the whole reason the counter is monotone:
/// the increment can only ever happen on the transition of one specific coin
/// from "untouched" to "touched", so standing on a coin, or walking back over
/// it, counts nothing.  That is iteration 2's plan requirement ("no re-trigger
/// when the player stands still on an already-collected coin").
fn collect_coins(
    mut counter: ResMut<CoinCounter>,
    player: Query<&Transform, With<Player>>,
    mut coins: Query<(&Transform, &mut Coin), Without<Player>>,
) {
    for player_transform in &player {
        let player_x = player_transform.translation.x;
        for (coin_transform, mut coin) in &mut coins {
            if !coin.collected && collects_at(coin_transform.translation.x, player_x) {
                coin.collected = true;
                counter.coins = counter.coins.saturating_add(1);
            }
        }
    }
}

/// Reaching the goal latches the win flag (PRD P3).
///
/// The write is **one-way**: `won` is only ever set, never cleared, so the
/// false -> true transition the criterion looks for can never be undone.  The
/// goal test is *independent* of the coins -- PRD P3 asks that reaching the goal
/// flips the flag, so a run that walks straight there must still win, and the
/// level simply places the goal past both coins so the natural walk collects
/// them on the way.
fn detect_win(
    mut win: ResMut<WinFlag>,
    player: Query<&Transform, With<Player>>,
    goal: Query<&Transform, With<Goal>>,
) {
    if win.won {
        return;
    }
    for player_transform in &player {
        let player_x = player_transform.translation.x;
        for goal_transform in &goal {
            if win_reached(goal_transform.translation.x, player_x) {
                win.won = true;
            }
        }
    }
}

/// The next wall-clock deadline (milliseconds after launch) that has not been
/// served yet, given `written` records already written.
///
/// The first [`LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS`] records use the fixed early schedule;
/// after that the interval is [`LIVENESS_INTERVAL_MS`].  Pure, so the schedule
/// is testable without waiting for it.
fn next_deadline_ms(written: u64) -> u64 {
    let fixed = LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS.len() as u64;
    if written < fixed {
        LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS[written as usize]
    } else {
        let last = LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS[LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS.len() - 1];
        last + LIVENESS_INTERVAL_MS * (written - fixed + 1)
    }
}

/// How many deadlines have come due by `elapsed_ms`, never fewer than `written`.
///
/// Computed in closed form rather than by stepping deadline by deadline, so a
/// process that was suspended for an hour (or a clock that jumped) cannot turn
/// this into a long loop.  Pure, so the catch-up rule is unit tested.
fn records_due(written: u64, elapsed_ms: u64) -> u64 {
    let fixed = LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS.len();
    let last = LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS[fixed - 1];
    let due = if elapsed_ms < last {
        LIVENESS_DEADLINES_MS
            .iter()
            .filter(|deadline| **deadline <= elapsed_ms)
            .count() as u64
    } else {
        fixed as u64 + (elapsed_ms - last) / LIVENESS_INTERVAL_MS
    };
    due.max(written)
}

/// Resolve the liveness evidence directory, preferring [`ARTIFACT_DIR_ENV`] when
/// it is set.  Kept pure (the environment is passed in) so it is testable.
fn liveness_dir(artifact_dir: Option<&str>) -> std::path::PathBuf {
    let relative = std::path::Path::new(LIVENESS_DIR_RELATIVE);
    let tail = relative.strip_prefix(".hoh").unwrap_or(relative);
    match artifact_dir {
        Some(dir) if !dir.is_empty() => std::path::Path::new(dir).join(tail),
        _ => relative.to_path_buf(),
    }
}

/// The evidence directory for *this* process: [`liveness_dir`] fed from the
/// environment.
fn resolved_liveness_dir() -> std::path::PathBuf {
    liveness_dir(std::env::var(ARTIFACT_DIR_ENV).ok().as_deref())
}

/// Append one line to `path`, creating it if needed, swallowing every error.
fn append_line(path: &std::path::Path, line: &str) {
    use std::io::Write;
    if let Ok(mut file) = std::fs::OpenOptions::new()
        .create(true)
        .append(true)
        .open(path)
    {
        let _ = writeln!(file, "{line}");
    }
}

/// Persist one record: the JSON line into [`LIVENESS_JSONL_NAME`] and the
/// `key=value` line into [`LIVENESS_LOG_NAME`].
///
/// Both sinks are attempted independently and neither can fail the caller: a
/// missing directory, a full disk or a locked file is ignored, because a
/// liveness probe must never be able to stall or fail the game it is proving
/// alive.
fn persist_record(dir: &std::path::Path, json: &str, text: &str) {
    if std::fs::create_dir_all(dir).is_err() && !dir.is_dir() {
        return;
    }
    append_line(&dir.join(LIVENESS_JSONL_NAME), json);
    append_line(&dir.join(LIVENESS_LOG_NAME), text);
}

/// The wall-clock milliseconds of "now", or 0 if the system clock is before the
/// epoch (which cannot fail the game either).
fn wall_ms() -> u128 {
    std::time::SystemTime::now()
        .duration_since(std::time::UNIX_EPOCH)
        .map(|elapsed| elapsed.as_millis())
        .unwrap_or(0)
}

/// The one line that says the process booted, written before the first frame.
///
/// It carries the game's frame counter and the process id, so the `raw/`
/// evidence area starts with a record proving *this* process reached startup.
fn announce_boot(frames: Res<FrameCounter>) {
    let pid = std::process::id();
    let wall = wall_ms();
    let json = format!(
        "{{\"probe\":\"hof_game\",\"event\":\"boot\",\"frame\":{},\"pid\":{},\"wall_ms\":{}}}",
        frames.frames, pid, wall
    );
    let text = format!(
        "hof_game boot frame={} pid={} wall_ms={}",
        frames.frames, pid, wall
    );
    eprintln!("{text}");
    persist_record(&resolved_liveness_dir(), &json, &text);
}

/// The persisted liveness record (plan gap S1).
///
/// Due on a **wall-clock** schedule ([`next_deadline_ms`]), so a record exists
/// seconds after launch whatever the frame rate does, and stamped with the
/// game's own [`FrameCounter`], so a reader can prove the game advanced rather
/// than the poller repeating itself.  It also carries the player transform, the
/// ground state, the coin counter and the win flag, which is what makes a late
/// record citable as "alive *and* still in the state the battery left it in".
///
/// Every failure mode is swallowed on purpose.  It observes; it never changes
/// behaviour.
fn liveness_probe(
    frames: Res<FrameCounter>,
    coins: Res<CoinCounter>,
    win: Res<WinFlag>,
    player: Query<(&Transform, &Grounded), With<Player>>,
    mut clock: ResMut<LivenessClock>,
) {
    let elapsed_ms = clock.started.elapsed().as_millis().min(u64::MAX as u128) as u64;
    let due = records_due(clock.written, elapsed_ms);
    if due <= clock.written {
        return;
    }
    // A process that was suspended for a long time has several deadlines behind
    // it; one record catches the schedule up (the values would be identical
    // anyway) instead of writing a burst of stale lines.
    clock.written = due;

    let pid = std::process::id();
    let wall = wall_ms();
    let frame = frames.frames;
    let player_state = player
        .iter()
        .next()
        .map(|(transform, grounded)| {
            (
                transform.translation.x,
                transform.translation.y,
                grounded.on_ground,
            )
        })
        .unwrap_or((f32::NAN, f32::NAN, false));
    let (x, y, on_ground) = player_state;

    let json = format!(
        "{{\"probe\":\"hof_game\",\"event\":\"liveness\",\"frame\":{frame},\"pid\":{pid},\
         \"wall_ms\":{wall},\"elapsed_ms\":{elapsed_ms},\"coins\":{},\"target\":{},\
         \"won\":{},\"x\":{x:.2},\"y\":{y:.2},\"on_ground\":{on_ground}}}",
        coins.coins, coins.target, win.won
    );
    let text = format!(
        "hof_game liveness frame={frame} pid={pid} wall_ms={wall} elapsed_ms={elapsed_ms} \
         coins={} target={} won={} x={x:.2} y={y:.2} on_ground={on_ground}",
        coins.coins, coins.target, win.won
    );
    eprintln!("{text}");
    persist_record(&resolved_liveness_dir(), &json, &text);
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    /// One 60 Hz game frame, the rate `main`'s schedule runner is set to.
    const DT: f32 = 1.0 / 60.0;

    /// The level from [`spawn_scene`], minus the ECS: the tests drive the very
    /// same helpers (`plan_velocity`, `step_motion`, `collects_at`,
    /// `win_reached`) the running systems call, so a behaviour that passes here
    /// is the behaviour the game has, not a copy of it.
    struct Sim {
        transform: Transform,
        velocity: Velocity,
        grounded: Grounded,
        latch: JumpLatch,
        coins: Vec<(f32, bool)>,
        counter: CoinCounter,
        win: WinFlag,
    }

    impl Sim {
        fn new() -> Self {
            Self {
                transform: Transform::from_xyz(0.0, GROUND_Y, 0.0),
                velocity: Velocity::default(),
                grounded: Grounded::default(),
                latch: JumpLatch::default(),
                coins: COIN_XS.iter().map(|x| (*x, false)).collect(),
                counter: CoinCounter {
                    coins: 0,
                    target: COIN_XS.len() as i64,
                },
                win: WinFlag::default(),
            }
        }

        /// One frame of the same pipeline the running game runs, in the same
        /// order: input, motion, pickup, goal.
        fn frame(&mut self, intent: InputIntent) {
            plan_velocity(&mut self.velocity, &mut self.grounded, &mut self.latch, intent);
            step_motion(&mut self.transform, &mut self.velocity, &mut self.grounded, DT);
            self.collect();
            self.reach_goal();
        }

        /// The pickup half of one frame, exactly like `collect_coins`.
        fn collect(&mut self) {
            let player_x = self.transform.translation.x;
            for (coin_x, collected) in &mut self.coins {
                if !*collected && collects_at(*coin_x, player_x) {
                    *collected = true;
                    self.counter.coins = self.counter.coins.saturating_add(1);
                }
            }
        }

        /// The goal half of one frame, exactly like `detect_win` (one-way).
        fn reach_goal(&mut self) {
            if !self.win.won && win_reached(GOAL_X, self.transform.translation.x) {
                self.win.won = true;
            }
        }
    }

    fn right() -> InputIntent {
        InputIntent {
            move_dir: 1,
            jump_pressed: false,
        }
    }

    fn left() -> InputIntent {
        InputIntent {
            move_dir: -1,
            jump_pressed: false,
        }
    }

    fn stop() -> InputIntent {
        InputIntent {
            move_dir: 0,
            jump_pressed: false,
        }
    }

    fn hold_jump() -> InputIntent {
        InputIntent {
            move_dir: 0,
            jump_pressed: true,
        }
    }

    #[test]
    fn held_input_moves_and_release_stops_without_inertia() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        for _ in 0..30 {
            sim.frame(right());
        }
        let moved = sim.transform.translation.x;
        assert!(
            moved > 90.0 && moved < 110.0,
            "30 frames at 200 px/s is 100 px, got {moved}"
        );

        // Release: level 0 must zero the velocity on the very first frame.
        for _ in 0..30 {
            sim.frame(stop());
        }
        assert_eq!(
            sim.transform.translation.x, moved,
            "released input must freeze x"
        );
        assert_eq!(sim.velocity.x, 0.0);
    }

    #[test]
    fn jump_rises_then_falls_and_lands() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        assert!(sim.grounded.on_ground, "the player stands before the press");

        let take_off_y = sim.transform.translation.y;

        // The very frame the press arrives: ground state off, rising.
        plan_velocity(
            &mut sim.velocity,
            &mut sim.grounded,
            &mut sim.latch,
            hold_jump(),
        );
        assert!(!sim.grounded.on_ground, "the press must clear the ground state");
        step_motion(&mut sim.transform, &mut sim.velocity, &mut sim.grounded, DT);
        assert!(
            sim.transform.translation.y > take_off_y,
            "the first airborne frame must already be higher"
        );

        // Keep holding: the latch means no second jump, and the player tracks a
        // full arc back to the ground.
        let mut trace = vec![sim.transform.translation.y];
        for _ in 0..60 {
            sim.frame(hold_jump());
            trace.push(sim.transform.translation.y);
        }

        let apex = trace.iter().cloned().fold(f32::MIN, f32::max);
        assert!(apex > take_off_y + 30.0, "the jump must rise: apex {apex}");
        let apex_at = trace.iter().position(|y| *y == apex).unwrap();
        assert!(apex_at > 0, "the apex is not the first frame");
        assert!(
            trace[apex_at..].iter().any(|y| *y < apex - 5.0),
            "the jump must come back down"
        );
        assert!(
            (trace[trace.len() - 1] - take_off_y).abs() < f32::EPSILON,
            "the jump must land back on the ground"
        );
        assert!(sim.grounded.on_ground, "and the ground state must be true again");
    }

    #[test]
    fn grounded_is_false_for_the_airborne_frames() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        // Rise phase: the first ~10 frames are still going up, so the player is
        // off the ground for all of them.
        sim.frame(hold_jump());
        for _ in 0..8 {
            assert!(
                !sim.grounded.on_ground,
                "the player is airborne while rising, y = {}",
                sim.transform.translation.y
            );
            assert!(sim.transform.translation.y > GROUND_Y);
            sim.frame(hold_jump());
        }
    }

    #[test]
    fn held_jump_is_one_jump_not_a_trampoline() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        let mut take_offs = 0;
        for _ in 0..120 {
            let was_grounded = sim.grounded.on_ground;
            plan_velocity(
                &mut sim.velocity,
                &mut sim.grounded,
                &mut sim.latch,
                hold_jump(),
            );
            if was_grounded && !sim.grounded.on_ground {
                take_offs += 1;
            }
            step_motion(&mut sim.transform, &mut sim.velocity, &mut sim.grounded, DT);
        }
        assert_eq!(take_offs, 1, "a held press is exactly one jump");
    }

    #[test]
    fn walking_right_collects_both_coins_monotonically() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 0, "the run starts at 0 coins");
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.target, 2, "the level holds two coins");

        let mut trace = Vec::new();
        let mut first_coin_at = None;
        let mut second_coin_at = None;
        for frame_index in 0..120 {
            sim.frame(right());
            trace.push(sim.counter.coins);
            if first_coin_at.is_none() && sim.counter.coins >= 1 {
                first_coin_at = Some(frame_index);
                assert!(sim.transform.translation.x >= COIN_X - COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS);
            }
            if second_coin_at.is_none() && sim.counter.coins >= 2 {
                second_coin_at = Some(frame_index);
                assert!(sim.transform.translation.x >= COIN2_X - COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS);
            }
        }

        let first = first_coin_at.expect("coin #1 is reachable on foot");
        let second = second_coin_at.expect("coin #2 is reachable on foot");
        assert!(first < second, "the coins are reached in their x order");
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 2, "two coins, counted twice");

        // Monotone: the sequence never decreases and steps one at a time.
        for pair in trace.windows(2) {
            assert!(pair[1] >= pair[0], "the count must never decrease: {trace:?}");
            assert!(pair[1] - pair[0] <= 1, "at most one coin per frame: {trace:?}");
        }

        // Walking back left never clears either value.
        for _ in 0..120 {
            sim.frame(left());
        }
        assert!(sim.transform.translation.x < COIN_X, "the player walked back");
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 2, "the coin counter never decreases");
    }

    #[test]
    fn the_first_leg_reads_one_and_the_second_leg_reads_two() {
        // The shape of the battery's drive: hold right, stop and read, hold
        // right again, read.  The stop lands inside coin #1's reach and clear of
        // coin #2's, so the read between the legs is exactly 1, and the second
        // leg walks through coin #2, making the next read 2.
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        for _ in 0..18 {
            sim.frame(right());
        }
        sim.frame(stop());
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 1, "the read after the first leg is 1");
        assert!(
            sim.transform.translation.x < COIN2_X - COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS,
            "x = {} must be short of coin #2's reach",
            sim.transform.translation.x
        );
        assert!(
            sim.transform.translation.x >= COIN_X - COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS,
            "x = {} must be inside coin #1's reach",
            sim.transform.translation.x
        );

        // The battery's idle wait must not change it.
        for _ in 0..10 {
            sim.frame(stop());
        }
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 1, "standing still must not re-count");

        for _ in 0..15 {
            sim.frame(right());
        }
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 2, "the read after the second leg is 2");
        assert!(sim.win.won, "and the same leg walks on to the goal");
        assert!(sim.grounded.on_ground, "reached standing on the ground");
    }

    #[test]
    fn one_walk_right_runs_the_whole_loop_in_one_session() {
        // This iteration's priority 3, as a test: a *single* continuous walk
        // right must take `coins` 0 -> 1 -> 2 and then flip `won` false -> true,
        // in that order, without a jump and without leaving the ground.  That is
        // what rules out "green while coins read 0".
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 0, "the run starts at 0 coins");
        assert!(!sim.win.won, "the run starts without the win");

        let mut first_pickup = None;
        let mut second_pickup = None;
        let mut won_at = None;
        for frame_index in 0..180 {
            sim.frame(right());
            if first_pickup.is_none() && sim.counter.coins == 1 {
                first_pickup = Some(frame_index);
            }
            if second_pickup.is_none() && sim.counter.coins == 2 {
                second_pickup = Some(frame_index);
            }
            if won_at.is_none() && sim.win.won {
                won_at = Some(frame_index);
            }
        }

        let first = first_pickup.expect("the walk reaches coin #1");
        let second = second_pickup.expect("the walk reaches coin #2");
        let win = won_at.expect("the walk reaches the goal");
        assert!(first < second, "coin #1 comes first");
        assert!(
            second <= win,
            "the win cannot be reached before the second coin: {second} > {win}"
        );
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 2, "both coins are counted");
        assert!(sim.win.won, "and the flag is true at the end of the walk");
        assert!(
            sim.transform.translation.x >= GOAL_X,
            "the player is at the goal: x = {}",
            sim.transform.translation.x
        );
        assert!(
            sim.grounded.on_ground,
            "the win happens standing on the walkable ground, no jump needed"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn standing_still_on_a_collected_coin_does_not_retrigger_it() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        // Walk on to the first coin and stop exactly on it.
        while sim.transform.translation.x < COIN_X {
            sim.frame(right());
        }
        sim.frame(stop());
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 1, "the first coin is counted once");

        // One second of standing exactly on the coin with the input released.
        for _ in 0..60 {
            sim.frame(stop());
        }
        assert_eq!(
            sim.counter.coins, 1,
            "standing still on a collected coin must not count it again"
        );
        assert!(
            collects_at(COIN_X, sim.transform.translation.x),
            "the player is still within reach of coin #1"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn the_goal_is_past_both_coins_and_the_win_is_one_way() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        assert!(!sim.win.won, "the run starts without the win");
        assert!(GOAL_X > COIN2_X, "the goal is past both coins");

        let mut won_at = None;
        for frame_index in 0..120 {
            sim.frame(right());
            if won_at.is_none() && sim.win.won {
                won_at = Some(frame_index);
                assert_eq!(
                    sim.counter.coins, 2,
                    "the win is only reachable after both coins"
                );
                assert!(sim.transform.translation.x >= GOAL_X);
            }
        }
        assert!(won_at.is_some(), "walking right must reach the goal");
        assert!(sim.grounded.on_ground, "and reach it standing on the ground");

        // One-way: walking back left never clears the flag.
        for _ in 0..120 {
            sim.frame(left());
        }
        assert!(sim.transform.translation.x < COIN_X, "the player did walk back");
        assert!(sim.win.won, "the win flag is one-way");
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 2, "and the count stays put");
    }

    #[test]
    fn nothing_is_collected_at_the_starting_position() {
        let mut sim = Sim::new();
        assert!(!collects_at(COIN_X, 0.0), "the start is clear of coin #1");
        assert!(!collects_at(COIN2_X, 0.0), "the start is clear of coin #2");
        assert!(!win_reached(GOAL_X, 0.0), "the start is short of the goal");
        sim.frame(stop());
        assert_eq!(sim.counter.coins, 0, "the run starts at 0 coins");
        assert!(!sim.win.won, "the run starts without the win");
    }

    #[test]
    fn the_two_coin_windows_are_disjoint_and_ordered() {
        // The whole point of the geometry: coin #1's reach window ends before
        // coin #2's begins, so no single position collects both at once and the
        // counter has to step 0 -> 1 -> 2.
        let coin1_far = COIN_X + COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS;
        let coin2_near = COIN2_X - COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS;
        assert!(
            coin1_far < coin2_near,
            "coin #1 reach ends at {coin1_far}, coin #2 begins at {coin2_near}"
        );
        assert!(COIN2_X > COIN_X, "the coins are ordered left to right");
        assert!(GOAL_X > COIN2_X, "the goal is beyond coin #2");
        assert!(
            COIN_PICKUP_RADIUS > MOVE_SPEED * MAX_STEP_SECONDS,
            "one frame's step must be smaller than the reach window"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn the_liveness_dir_prefers_the_rounds_artifact_dir() {
        // The launcher exports HOH_ARTIFACT_DIR; when it is there the records
        // must land under that directory's `deterministic/raw/`, and when it is
        // not the working-directory-relative path is used.  The liveness
        // evidence of gap S1 has to be in the round's own area either way.
        let from_env = liveness_dir(Some("F:/round/artifact"));
        assert_eq!(
            from_env,
            std::path::Path::new("F:/round/artifact")
                .join("deterministic")
                .join("raw")
        );
        assert_eq!(
            liveness_dir(None),
            std::path::Path::new(".hoh/deterministic/raw")
        );
        assert_eq!(
            liveness_dir(Some("")),
            std::path::Path::new(".hoh/deterministic/raw"),
            "an empty variable is the same as an unset one"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn the_liveness_schedule_has_early_and_late_deadlines() {
        // Early deadlines land inside the short E3 window; the late ones are the
        // record gap S1 asks for, so a reader can cite "still advancing frames
        // long after the battery's last step".
        assert_eq!(next_deadline_ms(0), 2_000, "the first record is early");
        assert_eq!(next_deadline_ms(6), 60_000, "the last fixed one is late");
        assert_eq!(
            next_deadline_ms(7),
            60_000 + LIVENESS_INTERVAL_MS,
            "after the fixed schedule it keeps repeating"
        );
        assert!(
            next_deadline_ms(6) >= 30_000,
            "there is a deadline several seconds after the early window"
        );

        // How many deadlines have come due.
        assert_eq!(records_due(0, 0), 0, "nothing is due at launch");
        assert_eq!(records_due(0, 1_999), 0);
        assert_eq!(records_due(0, 2_000), 1, "the first deadline is 2 s");
        assert_eq!(records_due(0, 5_000), 2, "2 s and 4 s are both due");
        assert_eq!(records_due(0, 60_000), 7, "all fixed deadlines are due");
        assert_eq!(records_due(0, 90_000), 8, "then one per interval");
        assert_eq!(
            records_due(3, 0),
            3,
            "a suspended process never rewinds the count"
        );
        assert_eq!(
            records_due(0, u64::MAX),
            7 + (u64::MAX - 60_000) / LIVENESS_INTERVAL_MS,
            "a huge clock jump is computed in closed form, not by stepping"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_record_lands_in_the_jsonl_and_the_log_and_carries_the_game_frame() {
        // The persisted record the plan's gap S1 asks for: both files must be
        // appended (not truncated) and the JSON line must carry the *game's*
        // frame counter, so the record is a claim about the game and not about
        // the poller.
        let dir = std::env::temp_dir().join("hof_game_liveness_test");
        let _ = std::fs::remove_dir_all(&dir);
        let json = "{\"probe\":\"hof_game\",\"event\":\"liveness\",\"frame\":720}";
        let text = "hof_game liveness frame=720 coins=2 won=true x=210.00";
        persist_record(&dir, json, text);
        persist_record(&dir, json, text);

        let jsonl = std::fs::read_to_string(dir.join(LIVENESS_JSONL_NAME)).unwrap();
        let log = std::fs::read_to_string(dir.join(LIVENESS_LOG_NAME)).unwrap();
        assert_eq!(jsonl.lines().count(), 2, "the JSONL record must append");
        assert_eq!(log.lines().count(), 2, "the text log must append too");
        assert!(jsonl.contains("\"frame\":720"), "the game frame is the stamp");
        assert!(log.contains("frame=720"), "the game frame is the stamp");
        let _ = std::fs::remove_dir_all(&dir);
    }
}
HOH_END_WRITE_FILE"}

